/-
Theses/B/Eff/JordanAlgebras.lean

Statements of eff.tex (thesis B, "Diamond, andthen, dagger"), line 2063:
the third example of an effectus in total form listed in the Examples
189aIII — the category `EJAᵒᵖ` of Euclidean Jordan algebras with positive
unit-preserving linear maps in the opposite direction.

Design:
* eff.tex prints no definition of a Euclidean Jordan algebra; it refers to
  the joint paper with van de Wetering.  `EuclideanJordanAlgebra` here is
  the classical *formally real* definition (Jordan–von Neumann–Wigner): a
  finite-dimensional real commutative algebra with a unit whose
  multiplication satisfies the Jordan identity and in which a sum of
  squares vanishes only if each summand does.  The Jordan identity is
  written exactly as Mathlib's `IsCommJordan.lmul_comm_rmul_rmul`, and
  formal reality exactly as the hypothesis of Mathlib's
  `IsFormallyReal.of_eq_zero_of_eq_zero_of_mul_self_add`; both bridges are
  `EuclideanJordanAlgebra.isCommJordan` and
  `EuclideanJordanAlgebra.isFormallyReal` below.  The *Euclidean* form of
  the definition — a positive definite bilinear form with
  `B (x * y) z = B y (x * z)`, i.e. the trace form — is carried too, in
  the direction that is needed: `formallyReal_of_form` derives formal
  reality from such a form, so `EJAObj.ofForm` turns the textbook data
  into an object of the category.  The converse (that a formally real
  finite-dimensional Jordan algebra carries such a form) is the
  Jordan–von Neumann–Wigner theorem and is not formalized; nothing here
  uses it.
* Like `OrderUnitSpace` in `OrderUnit.lean`, the class is a *mixin*, here
  over `AddCommGroup`, `Module ℝ`, `Mul`, `One` and `PartialOrder`, so
  that `ULift`s, products and the one-point algebra carry exactly
  Mathlib's own algebra and order.  The order is not extra data: the
  axiom `le_iff` says that `x ≤ y` holds precisely when `y - x` is a sum
  of squares, which is the order of the cone of squares — and in a
  Euclidean Jordan algebra a sum of squares *is* again a square, so this is
  the cone of squares: that is `eja_isSumSq_iff_exists_sq`, a corollary of
  the spectral theorem proved below.  That this relation is antisymmetric
  is *not* an extra assumption: it is formal reality,
  `eja_eq_zero_of_isSumSq_of_neg`.
* The mathematical work of the file is `eja_exists_isSumSq_nsmul_one_sub`:
  the unit of a Euclidean Jordan algebra is an order unit, i.e. every `x`
  satisfies `x ≤ n · 1` for some natural `n`.  This is the one point where
  finite-dimensionality and formal reality are both used, and it is proved
  by separation rather than by spectral theory: if the set of elements
  bounded by a multiple of `1` were not everything, a finite-dimensional
  Hahn–Banach argument would give a non-zero linear `f` with `f ≤ 0` on
  that set, hence `f(a * a) ≥ 0` for all `a` and `f 1 = 0`; expanding
  `0 ≤ f((a + t · 1) * (a + t · 1)) = f(a * a) + 2t · f(a)` for all real
  `t` forces `f a = 0` for every `a`.  With that, `toOrderUnitSpace` makes
  every Euclidean Jordan algebra an order unit space in the sense of
  `OrderUnit.lean`, with the cone of (sums of) squares and the Jordan unit
  as order unit.
* The second block of the file is the **spectral theorem for a single
  element**, `eja_spectral`: every `x` is a real linear combination of
  finitely many pairwise orthogonal non-zero idempotents summing to `1`.
  Its steps are power-associativity (`eja_pow_mul_pow`) — the subalgebra
  generated by one element is associative — from Mathlib's full
  linearisation of the Jordan identity (`eja_lin`); the resulting
  multiplicative evaluation map `ℝ[X] →ₗ[ℝ] V` (`ejaEv`, `ejaEv_mul`); the
  minimal polynomial (`eja_exists_minpoly`), which formal reality forces to
  split into *distinct* linear factors (`eja_minpoly_split`,
  `eja_minpoly_nodup`); and the Lagrange basis polynomials of its roots,
  evaluated at `x`, which are the spectral idempotents.
* On top of the spectral theorem the file builds the **trace form**
  `B x y = tr (L (x * y))` (`ejaB`).  It is associative (`ejaB_assoc`)
  because `⁅L a, L b⁆` is a derivation (`eja_der`, two instances of the
  linearised identity), and positive definite (`ejaB_self_pos`) because the
  trace of a non-zero idempotent is positive (`eja_tr_idem_pos`, via the
  Peirce projections `ejaPone` and `ejaPhalf`).  That yields the three
  corollaries the rest of the file uses: **every sum of squares is a
  square** (`eja_isSumSq_iff_exists_sq`, hence `eja_nonneg_iff_exists_sq`),
  **an element is in the unit interval exactly when its spectral values
  are** (`eja_ortho_unit_interval_iff`), and **the states separate the
  points** (`eja_exists_state_ne_zero`).
* `EJA` is then the full subcategory of `OUS` spanned by the order unit
  spaces whose order and unit come from a Euclidean Jordan structure
  (`EJAObj.of` and `EJAObj.ofForm` put every Euclidean Jordan algebra
  there, and fullness makes its morphisms the positive unital maps), in
  exactly the way `CvN` is the full subcategory of `vN` spanned by the
  commutative algebras (`VNExamples.lean`).  `ℝᵤ`, the one-point algebra
  and a product of Euclidean Jordan algebras are Euclidean Jordan
  algebras, so the concrete presentation `ousPres` of `OUSᵒᵖ` restricts,
  and the three axioms of 180I are pulled back along the fully faithful
  inclusion `EJAᵒᵖ ⥤ OUSᵒᵖ`; no order-unit theory is redone.
* Of 190V, the two correspondences are carried: the predicates of `EJAᵒᵖ`
  are the effects `0 ≤ a ≤ 1` (`eja_pred_effect`, with truth, zero and the
  orthosupplement pinned) and the states are the positive unit-preserving
  functionals (`eja_stat_state`).  Not formalized: the ⋄-effectus clause of
  206III for `EJAᵒᵖ` (see the comment at the end of the file), the two
  separation claims and the scalars of 190V, and the &- and †-structures of
  eff.tex 209I and 213I.
-/
import Theses.B.Eff.OrderUnit
import Mathlib.Algebra.Jordan.Basic
import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Lagrange
import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Trace
import Mathlib.Analysis.Polynomial.Factorization

set_option warn.classDefReducibility false

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace Theses.B.Eff

universe u v

noncomputable section

/-! ## Sums of squares in `ULift`s and products

Two transfer lemmas for Mathlib's `IsSumSq`, needed because the objects of
`OUS` live in a single universe (`ULift`) and because the binary
coproducts of `OUSᵒᵖ` are products. -/

/-- A `ULift` of an element is a sum of squares exactly when the element
is. -/
theorem isSumSq_ulift_iff {α : Type u} [AddZeroClass α] [Mul α]
    {x : ULift.{v} α} : IsSumSq x ↔ IsSumSq x.down := by
  constructor
  · intro h
    induction h with
    | zero => exact IsSumSq.zero
    | @sq_add a s _ ih => exact IsSumSq.sq_add a.down ih
  · intro h
    have key : ∀ y : α, IsSumSq y → IsSumSq (ULift.up.{v} y) := by
      intro y hy
      induction hy with
      | zero => exact IsSumSq.zero
      | @sq_add a s _ ih => exact IsSumSq.sq_add (ULift.up.{v} a) ih
    simpa using key x.down h

/-- The first component of a sum of squares in a product is a sum of
squares. -/
theorem isSumSq_fst {α : Type u} {β : Type v} [AddZeroClass α] [Mul α]
    [AddZeroClass β] [Mul β] {p : α × β} (h : IsSumSq p) : IsSumSq p.1 := by
  induction h with
  | zero => exact IsSumSq.zero
  | @sq_add a s _ ih => exact IsSumSq.sq_add a.1 ih

/-- The second component of a sum of squares in a product is a sum of
squares. -/
theorem isSumSq_snd {α : Type u} {β : Type v} [AddZeroClass α] [Mul α]
    [AddZeroClass β] [Mul β] {p : α × β} (h : IsSumSq p) : IsSumSq p.2 := by
  induction h with
  | zero => exact IsSumSq.zero
  | @sq_add a s _ ih => exact IsSumSq.sq_add a.2 ih

/-- A sum of squares in the left factor, placed in a product. -/
theorem isSumSq_inl {α : Type u} {β : Type v} [AddZeroClass α] [Mul α]
    [AddZeroClass β] [Mul β] (hz : (0 : β) * (0 : β) = 0) {a : α}
    (h : IsSumSq a) : IsSumSq ((a, 0) : α × β) := by
  induction h with
  | zero => exact IsSumSq.zero
  | @sq_add b s _ ih =>
    have he : ((b * b + s, (0 : β)) : α × β)
        = ((b, (0 : β)) : α × β) * ((b, (0 : β)) : α × β) + ((s, (0 : β)) : α × β) :=
      Prod.ext rfl (by simp [hz])
    rw [he]
    exact IsSumSq.sq_add _ ih

/-- A sum of squares in the right factor, placed in a product. -/
theorem isSumSq_inr {α : Type u} {β : Type v} [AddZeroClass α] [Mul α]
    [AddZeroClass β] [Mul β] (hz : (0 : α) * (0 : α) = 0) {b : β}
    (h : IsSumSq b) : IsSumSq (((0 : α), b) : α × β) := by
  induction h with
  | zero => exact IsSumSq.zero
  | @sq_add c s _ ih =>
    have he : (((0 : α), c * c + s) : α × β)
        = (((0 : α), c) : α × β) * ((0 : α), c) + (((0 : α), s) : α × β) :=
      Prod.ext (by simp [hz]) rfl
    rw [he]
    exact IsSumSq.sq_add _ ih

/-- **Formal reality makes the cone of sums of squares proper**: if `x`
and `y` are sums of squares with `x + y = 0`, then `x = 0`.  Stated with
the two algebraic facts as hypotheses, since it is used both inside the
class `EuclideanJordanAlgebra` and to build the partial order that the
class asks for. -/
theorem isSumSq_eq_zero_of_add {V : Type u} [AddCommGroup V] [Mul V]
    (hz : (0 : V) * (0 : V) = 0)
    (hfr : ∀ {a s : V}, IsSumSq s → a * a + s = 0 → a = 0)
    {x y : V} (h₁ : IsSumSq x) (h₂ : IsSumSq y) (h : x + y = 0) : x = 0 := by
  induction h₁ generalizing y with
  | zero => rfl
  | @sq_add a s hs ih =>
    have hsy : IsSumSq (s + y) := hs.add h₂
    have ha : a = 0 := by
      refine hfr hsy ?_
      rw [← add_assoc]
      exact h
    have haa : a * a = (0 : V) := by rw [ha]; exact hz
    have hsy0 : s + y = 0 := by
      have h' := h
      rw [haa, zero_add] at h'
      exact h'
    have hs0 : s = 0 := ih h₂ hsy0
    rw [haa, hs0, add_zero]

/-- The order of the cone of sums of squares is a partial order as soon as
the algebra is formally real; this is the order the `le_iff` axiom of
`EuclideanJordanAlgebra` demands, so that axiom constrains the order
rather than adding an assumption. -/
def sumSqPartialOrder (V : Type u) [AddCommGroup V] [Mul V]
    (hz : (0 : V) * (0 : V) = 0)
    (hfr : ∀ {a s : V}, IsSumSq s → a * a + s = 0 → a = 0) : PartialOrder V where
  le x y := IsSumSq (y - x)
  le_refl x := by
    show IsSumSq (x - x)
    simp
  le_trans x y z hxy hyz := by
    show IsSumSq (z - x)
    have he : z - x = (z - y) + (y - x) := by abel
    rw [he]
    exact IsSumSq.add hyz hxy
  le_antisymm x y hxy hyx := by
    have h₁ : IsSumSq (y - x) := hxy
    have h₂ : IsSumSq (x - y) := hyx
    have h : (y - x) + (x - y) = 0 := by abel
    have h0 : y - x = 0 := isSumSq_eq_zero_of_add hz hfr h₁ h₂ h
    exact (sub_eq_zero.mp h0).symm

/-! ## Euclidean Jordan algebras (189aIII) -/

/-- **189aIII** (`effexamplesintro`, eff.tex:2063, Examples): a **Euclidean
Jordan algebra**.

eff.tex prints no definition (it points at the joint paper with van de
Wetering); this is the classical *formally real* one: a finite-dimensional
real vector space with a commutative bilinear product satisfying the
Jordan identity `(a * b) * (a * a) = a * (b * (a * a))`, a unit for it,
and formal reality — `a * a + s = 0` with `s` a sum of squares forces
`a = 0`.  By the Jordan–von Neumann–Wigner theorem these are exactly the
algebras carrying an inner product with `⟪x * y, z⟫ = ⟪y, x * z⟫` (the
trace form), which is the definition usually spelled out; that equivalence
is not formalized here and is not used.

Stated as a mixin over `AddCommGroup X`, `Module ℝ X`, `Mul X`, `One X`
and `PartialOrder X`, in the style of `OrderUnitSpace`: the last axiom
`le_iff` says the order is the one of the cone of sums of squares. -/
class EuclideanJordanAlgebra (V : Type u) [AddCommGroup V] [Module ℝ V]
    [Mul V] [One V] [PartialOrder V] : Prop where
  /-- the Jordan product is commutative -/
  protected mul_comm : ∀ a b : V, a * b = b * a
  /-- ... and additive in its second (hence, by commutativity, either)
  argument -/
  protected mul_add : ∀ a b c : V, a * (b + c) = a * b + a * c
  /-- ... and real-homogeneous -/
  protected smul_mul : ∀ (r : ℝ) (a b : V), (r • a) * b = r • (a * b)
  /-- `1` is a unit for the Jordan product -/
  protected one_mul : ∀ a : V, 1 * a = a
  /-- the **Jordan identity**, verbatim Mathlib's
  `IsCommJordan.lmul_comm_rmul_rmul` -/
  protected lmul_comm_rmul_rmul : ∀ a b : V, a * b * (a * a) = a * (b * (a * a))
  /-- the algebra is finite-dimensional -/
  protected finiteDimensional : FiniteDimensional ℝ V
  /-- the algebra is **formally real**, verbatim the hypothesis of
  Mathlib's `IsFormallyReal.of_eq_zero_of_eq_zero_of_mul_self_add` -/
  protected formallyReal : ∀ {a s : V}, IsSumSq s → a * a + s = 0 → a = 0
  /-- the order is that of the cone of sums of squares -/
  protected le_iff : ∀ x y : V, x ≤ y ↔ IsSumSq (y - x)

namespace EuclideanJordanAlgebra

section Basic

variable {V : Type u} [AddCommGroup V] [Module ℝ V] [Mul V] [One V]
  [PartialOrder V] [EuclideanJordanAlgebra V]

instance toFiniteDimensional : FiniteDimensional ℝ V :=
  EuclideanJordanAlgebra.finiteDimensional

theorem eja_mul_comm (a b : V) : a * b = b * a := EuclideanJordanAlgebra.mul_comm a b

theorem eja_mul_add (a b c : V) : a * (b + c) = a * b + a * c :=
  EuclideanJordanAlgebra.mul_add a b c

theorem eja_add_mul (a b c : V) : (a + b) * c = a * c + b * c := by
  rw [eja_mul_comm, eja_mul_add, eja_mul_comm c a, eja_mul_comm c b]

/-- The full expansion of a product of two sums, used for the square of
`a + t · 1`. -/
theorem eja_add_mul_add (a b c d : V) :
    (a + b) * (c + d) = a * c + a * d + (b * c + b * d) := by
  rw [eja_add_mul, eja_mul_add, eja_mul_add]

theorem eja_smul_mul (r : ℝ) (a b : V) : (r • a) * b = r • (a * b) :=
  EuclideanJordanAlgebra.smul_mul r a b

theorem eja_mul_smul (r : ℝ) (a b : V) : a * (r • b) = r • (a * b) := by
  rw [eja_mul_comm, eja_smul_mul, eja_mul_comm]

theorem eja_one_mul (a : V) : 1 * a = a := EuclideanJordanAlgebra.one_mul a

theorem eja_mul_one (a : V) : a * 1 = a := by rw [eja_mul_comm, eja_one_mul]

theorem eja_zero_mul (a : V) : (0 : V) * a = 0 := by
  have h : (0 : V) * a + 0 * a = 0 * a + 0 := by
    rw [← eja_add_mul, add_zero, add_zero]
  exact add_left_cancel h

theorem eja_mul_zero (a : V) : a * (0 : V) = 0 := by
  rw [eja_mul_comm, eja_zero_mul]

theorem eja_mul_sub (a b c : V) : a * (b - c) = a * b - a * c := by
  have h : a * (b - c) + a * c = a * b := by
    rw [← eja_mul_add]
    congr 1
    abel
  exact eq_sub_of_add_eq h

theorem eja_sub_mul (a b c : V) : (a - b) * c = a * c - b * c := by
  rw [eja_mul_comm, eja_mul_sub, eja_mul_comm c a, eja_mul_comm c b]

/-- The Jordan identity of an `EuclideanJordanAlgebra` is Mathlib's: with
the commutative magma structure of the product, the algebra satisfies
`IsCommJordan`. -/
def commMagma (V : Type u) [AddCommGroup V] [Module ℝ V] [Mul V] [One V]
    [PartialOrder V] [EuclideanJordanAlgebra V] : CommMagma V where
  mul := (· * ·)
  mul_comm := eja_mul_comm

theorem isCommJordan : @IsCommJordan V (commMagma V) := by
  have h := EuclideanJordanAlgebra.lmul_comm_rmul_rmul (V := V)
  exact @IsCommJordan.mk V (commMagma V) h

/-- The additive and multiplicative structure of a Euclidean Jordan
algebra is a non-unital non-associative ring; this is what lets Mathlib's
`IsFormallyReal` machinery apply. -/
def nonUnitalNonAssocRing (V : Type u) [AddCommGroup V] [Module ℝ V] [Mul V]
    [One V] [PartialOrder V] [EuclideanJordanAlgebra V] :
    NonUnitalNonAssocRing V :=
  { (inferInstance : AddCommGroup V), (inferInstance : Mul V) with
    left_distrib := eja_mul_add
    right_distrib := eja_add_mul
    zero_mul := eja_zero_mul
    mul_zero := eja_mul_zero }

/-- **A Euclidean Jordan algebra is formally real** in the sense of
Mathlib's `IsFormallyReal`. -/
theorem isFormallyReal : IsFormallyReal V := by
  have key : ∀ {a s : V}, IsSumSq s → a * a + s = 0 → a = 0 :=
    fun hs h => EuclideanJordanAlgebra.formallyReal hs h
  let _ := nonUnitalNonAssocRing V
  exact IsFormallyReal.of_eq_zero_of_eq_zero_of_mul_self_add key

/-- **The cone of sums of squares is proper**: if both `x` and `-x` are
sums of squares then `x = 0`.  This is formal reality, and it is what
makes the order of `le_iff` antisymmetric — so requiring a `PartialOrder`
in the class is no extra assumption. -/
theorem eja_eq_zero_of_isSumSq_add {x y : V} (h₁ : IsSumSq x) (h₂ : IsSumSq y)
    (h : x + y = 0) : x = 0 :=
  isSumSq_eq_zero_of_add (eja_zero_mul 0)
    (fun hs hh => EuclideanJordanAlgebra.formallyReal hs hh) h₁ h₂ h

theorem eja_eq_zero_of_isSumSq_of_neg {x : V} (h₁ : IsSumSq x)
    (h₂ : IsSumSq (-x)) : x = 0 :=
  eja_eq_zero_of_isSumSq_add h₁ h₂ (add_neg_cancel x)

/-! ### The cone of sums of squares -/

theorem eja_le_iff (x y : V) : x ≤ y ↔ IsSumSq (y - x) :=
  EuclideanJordanAlgebra.le_iff x y

theorem eja_nonneg_iff (x : V) : 0 ≤ x ↔ IsSumSq x := by
  rw [eja_le_iff]
  simp

/-- A non-negative multiple of a sum of squares is a sum of squares:
`r · (a * a) = (√r · a) * (√r · a)`. -/
theorem eja_isSumSq_smul {r : ℝ} (hr : 0 ≤ r) {s : V} (hs : IsSumSq s) :
    IsSumSq (r • s) := by
  induction hs with
  | zero => simp
  | @sq_add a s _ ih =>
    have hsq : (Real.sqrt r • a) * (Real.sqrt r • a) = r • (a * a) := by
      rw [eja_smul_mul, eja_mul_smul, smul_smul, Real.mul_self_sqrt hr]
    have he : r • (a * a + s) = (Real.sqrt r • a) * (Real.sqrt r • a) + r • s := by
      rw [hsq, smul_add]
    rw [he]
    exact IsSumSq.sq_add _ ih

/-- `1` is a sum of squares. -/
theorem eja_isSumSq_one : IsSumSq (1 : V) := by
  have h := IsSumSq.mul_self (1 : V)
  rwa [eja_mul_one] at h

/-- A non-negative multiple of `1` is a sum of squares. -/
theorem eja_isSumSq_smul_one {r : ℝ} (hr : 0 ≤ r) : IsSumSq (r • (1 : V)) :=
  eja_isSumSq_smul hr eja_isSumSq_one

/-- `4 · x = (x + 1) * (x + 1) - (x - 1) * (x - 1)`: every element is a
difference of two squares, up to a positive scalar. -/
theorem eja_four_smul (x : V) :
    (4 : ℝ) • x = (x + 1) * (x + 1) - (x - 1) * (x - 1) := by
  have h₁ : (x + 1) * (x + 1) = x * x + (x + x) + 1 := by
    rw [eja_add_mul_add, eja_mul_one, eja_one_mul, eja_mul_one]
    abel
  have h₂ : (x - 1) * (x - 1) = x * x - (x + x) + 1 := by
    rw [eja_sub_mul, eja_mul_sub, eja_mul_sub, eja_mul_one, eja_one_mul,
      eja_mul_one]
    abel
  have h₄ : (4 : ℝ) • x = x + x + (x + x) := by
    rw [show (4 : ℝ) = 2 + 2 by norm_num, add_smul, two_smul]
  rw [h₁, h₂, h₄]
  abel

end Basic

/-! ### Power-associativity -/

/-- The commutative non-unital non-associative ring structure of a Euclidean
Jordan algebra, used only to borrow Mathlib's linearisation of the Jordan
identity. -/
def nonUnitalNonAssocCommRing (W : Type u) [AddCommGroup W] [Module ℝ W] [Mul W]
    [One W] [PartialOrder W] [EuclideanJordanAlgebra W] :
    NonUnitalNonAssocCommRing W :=
  have h : ∀ a b : W, a * b = b * a := eja_mul_comm
  { nonUnitalNonAssocRing W with mul_comm := h }

/-- Mathlib's full linearisation of the Jordan identity, applied to a vector. -/
theorem two_nsmul_lin_aux {W : Type u} [NonUnitalNonAssocCommRing W] [IsCommJordan W]
    (a b c w : W) :
    (2 : ℕ) • (a * (b * c * w) - b * c * (a * w) + (b * (c * a * w) - c * a * (b * w))
      + (c * (a * b * w) - a * b * (c * w))) = 0 :=
  congrArg (fun f : AddMonoid.End W => f w) (two_nsmul_lie_lmul_lmul_add_add_eq_zero a b c)

section Spectral

variable {V : Type u} [AddCommGroup V] [Module ℝ V] [Mul V] [One V]
  [PartialOrder V] [EuclideanJordanAlgebra V]

omit [Mul V] [One V] [PartialOrder V] [EuclideanJordanAlgebra V] in
/-- A Euclidean Jordan algebra is a real vector space, so it is torsion free. -/
theorem eja_eq_zero_of_nsmul {n : ℕ} (hn : n ≠ 0) {y : V} (h : n • y = 0) : y = 0 := by
  have h2 : ((n : ℕ) : ℝ) • y = 0 := by rwa [Nat.cast_smul_eq_nsmul]
  rcases smul_eq_zero.mp h2 with h3 | h3
  · exact absurd (Nat.cast_eq_zero.mp h3) hn
  · exact h3

/-- **The linearised Jordan identity**: the full polarisation of
`⁅L a, L (a * a)⁆ = 0` in `a`, written out on a vector `w`.  This is Mathlib's
`two_nsmul_lie_lmul_lmul_add_add_eq_zero`, with the factor `2` cancelled. -/
theorem eja_lin (a b c w : V) :
    a * (b * c * w) - b * c * (a * w) + (b * (c * a * w) - c * a * (b * w))
      + (c * (a * b * w) - a * b * (c * w)) = 0 := by
  have hcj : ∀ x y : V, x * y * (x * x) = x * (y * (x * x)) :=
    EuclideanJordanAlgebra.lmul_comm_rmul_rmul
  refine eja_eq_zero_of_nsmul (n := 2) two_ne_zero ?_
  let _inst := nonUnitalNonAssocCommRing V
  have _inst2 : IsCommJordan V := ⟨hcj⟩
  exact two_nsmul_lin_aux a b c w

/-- **`⁅L a, L b⁆` is a derivation**: two instances of the linearised Jordan
identity, added, leave exactly the Leibniz rule for `⁅L a, L b⁆`.  This is the
only consequence of the Jordan identity that the trace form needs. -/
theorem eja_der (a b c d : V) :
    a * (b * (c * d)) - b * (a * (c * d))
      = c * (a * (b * d)) - c * (b * (a * d)) + (d * (a * (b * c)) - d * (b * (a * c))) := by
  have h1 := eja_lin a c d b
  have h2 := eja_lin b c d a
  simp only [eja_mul_comm] at h1 h2 ⊢
  linear_combination (norm := module) h1 - h2

/-- The powers of an element: `ejaPow x 0 = 1` and `ejaPow x (n+1) = x * ejaPow x n`. -/
def ejaPow (x : V) : ℕ → V
  | 0 => 1
  | (n + 1) => x * ejaPow x n

omit [AddCommGroup V] [Module ℝ V] [PartialOrder V] [EuclideanJordanAlgebra V] in
@[simp] theorem ejaPow_zero (x : V) : ejaPow x 0 = 1 := rfl

omit [AddCommGroup V] [Module ℝ V] [PartialOrder V] [EuclideanJordanAlgebra V] in
theorem ejaPow_succ (x : V) (n : ℕ) : ejaPow x (n + 1) = x * ejaPow x n := rfl

@[simp] theorem ejaPow_one (x : V) : ejaPow x 1 = x := by
  rw [ejaPow_succ, ejaPow_zero, eja_mul_one]

theorem ejaPow_two (x : V) : ejaPow x 2 = x * x := by
  rw [ejaPow_succ, ejaPow_one]

/-- The commutator of the multiplications by `x ^ k` and `x ^ (M - k)`, applied
to `w`; the vehicle of the power-associativity induction. -/
def ejaCm (x : V) (M k : ℕ) (w : V) : V :=
  ejaPow x k * (ejaPow x (M - k) * w) - ejaPow x (M - k) * (ejaPow x k * w)

omit [Module ℝ V] [PartialOrder V] [EuclideanJordanAlgebra V] in
theorem ejaCm_neg (x : V) {M k : ℕ} (h : k ≤ M) (w : V) :
    ejaCm x M (M - k) w = - ejaCm x M k w := by
  have e : M - (M - k) = k := by omega
  simp only [ejaCm, e]
  abel

theorem ejaCm_sum (x : V) {M : ℕ}
    (hA : ∀ i j : ℕ, i + j ≤ M - 1 → ejaPow x i * ejaPow x j = ejaPow x (i + j))
    (w : V) {p q r : ℕ} (hp : 1 ≤ p) (hq : 1 ≤ q) (hr : 1 ≤ r) (hs : p + q + r = M) :
    ejaCm x M p w + ejaCm x M q w + ejaCm x M r w = 0 := by
  have h := eja_lin (ejaPow x p) (ejaPow x q) (ejaPow x r) w
  rw [hA q r (by omega), hA r p (by omega), hA p q (by omega)] at h
  have e1 : q + r = M - p := by omega
  have e2 : r + p = M - q := by omega
  have e3 : p + q = M - r := by omega
  rw [e1, e2, e3] at h
  simp only [ejaCm]
  linear_combination (norm := module) h

theorem ejaCm_eq_zero (x : V) {M : ℕ} (hM : 3 ≤ M)
    (hA : ∀ i j : ℕ, i + j ≤ M - 1 → ejaPow x i * ejaPow x j = ejaPow x (i + j))
    (w : V) {k : ℕ} (hk1 : 1 ≤ k) (hk2 : k ≤ M - 1) :
    ejaCm x M k w = 0 := by
  have hstep : ∀ j : ℕ, 1 ≤ j → j ≤ M - 2 →
      ejaCm x M (j + 1) w = ejaCm x M 1 w + ejaCm x M j w := by
    intro j hj1 hj2
    have hsum := ejaCm_sum x hA w (p := 1) (q := j) (r := M - 1 - j)
      le_rfl hj1 (by omega) (by omega)
    have e : M - 1 - j = M - (j + 1) := by omega
    rw [e, ejaCm_neg x (k := j + 1) (by omega) w] at hsum
    linear_combination (norm := module) -hsum
  have hmul : ∀ j : ℕ, j ≤ M - 1 → 1 ≤ j → ejaCm x M j w = j • ejaCm x M 1 w := by
    intro j
    induction j with
    | zero => intro _ h; exact absurd h (by omega)
    | succ n ih =>
      intro hn hn1
      rcases Nat.eq_zero_or_pos n with rfl | hn0
      · simp
      · rw [hstep n hn0 (by omega), ih (by omega) hn0, succ_nsmul]
        abel
  have hM1 : ejaCm x M (M - 1) w = - ejaCm x M 1 w := by
    have := ejaCm_neg x (M := M) (k := 1) (by omega) w
    simpa using this
  have hlast : M • ejaCm x M 1 w = 0 := by
    have h1 : ejaCm x M (M - 1) w = (M - 1) • ejaCm x M 1 w :=
      hmul (M - 1) le_rfl (by omega)
    have e : M - 1 + 1 = M := by omega
    have h3 : (M - 1) • ejaCm x M 1 w + ejaCm x M 1 w = 0 := by
      rw [← h1, hM1]; abel
    rw [← e, succ_nsmul]
    exact h3
  have hc1 : ejaCm x M 1 w = 0 := eja_eq_zero_of_nsmul (by omega) hlast
  rw [hmul k hk2 hk1, hc1, smul_zero]

theorem eja_pow_aux (x : V) : ∀ N : ℕ,
    (∀ i j : ℕ, i + j ≤ N → ejaPow x i * ejaPow x j = ejaPow x (i + j)) ∧
    (∀ i j : ℕ, i + j ≤ N → ∀ w : V,
      ejaPow x i * (ejaPow x j * w) = ejaPow x j * (ejaPow x i * w)) := by
  intro N
  induction N with
  | zero =>
    refine ⟨?_, ?_⟩
    · intro i j hij
      have hi : i = 0 := by omega
      have hj : j = 0 := by omega
      subst hi; subst hj
      simp [eja_one_mul]
    · intro i j hij w
      have hi : i = 0 := by omega
      have hj : j = 0 := by omega
      subst hi; subst hj
      rfl
  | succ N ih =>
    obtain ⟨hA, hB⟩ := ih
    have hB' : ∀ i j : ℕ, i + j ≤ N + 1 → ∀ w : V,
        ejaPow x i * (ejaPow x j * w) = ejaPow x j * (ejaPow x i * w) := by
      intro i j hij w
      rcases Nat.lt_or_ge (i + j) (N + 1) with h | h
      · exact hB i j (by omega) w
      have hij' : i + j = N + 1 := by omega
      rcases Nat.eq_zero_or_pos i with rfl | hi
      · simp [eja_one_mul]
      rcases Nat.eq_zero_or_pos j with rfl | hj
      · simp [eja_one_mul]
      rcases Nat.lt_or_ge (N + 1) 3 with h3 | h3
      · have hi1 : i = 1 := by omega
        have hj1 : j = 1 := by omega
        subst hi1; subst hj1
        rfl
      · have hcm : ejaCm x (N + 1) i w = 0 := by
          refine ejaCm_eq_zero x h3 (fun a b hab => hA a b (by omega)) w hi (by omega)
        have e : N + 1 - i = j := by omega
        simp only [ejaCm, e] at hcm
        exact sub_eq_zero.mp hcm
    refine ⟨?_, hB'⟩
    intro i j hij
    rcases Nat.lt_or_ge (i + j) (N + 1) with h | h
    · exact hA i j (by omega)
    have hij' : i + j = N + 1 := by omega
    rcases Nat.eq_zero_or_pos j with rfl | hj
    · simp [eja_mul_one]
    rcases Nat.eq_or_lt_of_le hj with h1 | h1
    · have hj1 : j = 1 := h1.symm
      subst hj1
      rw [ejaPow_one, eja_mul_comm, ← ejaPow_succ]
    · have e : j - 1 + 1 = j := by omega
      have h1' : ejaPow x i * ejaPow x j = ejaPow x i * (ejaPow x 1 * ejaPow x (j - 1)) := by
        rw [ejaPow_one, ← ejaPow_succ, e]
      have h2 := hB i 1 (by omega) (ejaPow x (j - 1))
      have h3 := hA i (j - 1) (by omega)
      rw [h1', h2, h3, ejaPow_one, ← ejaPow_succ]
      congr 1
      omega

/-- **Power-associativity**: the subalgebra generated by a single element of a
Euclidean Jordan algebra is associative. -/
theorem eja_pow_mul_pow (x : V) (m n : ℕ) :
    ejaPow x m * ejaPow x n = ejaPow x (m + n) :=
  (eja_pow_aux x (m + n)).1 m n le_rfl

/-! ### The trace form -/

/-- Multiplication by `a`, as a linear endomorphism of `V`. -/
def ejaLm (a : V) : V →ₗ[ℝ] V where
  toFun w := a * w
  map_add' := eja_mul_add a
  map_smul' r w := eja_mul_smul r a w

@[simp] theorem ejaLm_apply (a w : V) : ejaLm a w = a * w := rfl

theorem ejaLm_add (a b : V) : ejaLm (a + b) = ejaLm a + ejaLm b := by
  ext w; simp [eja_add_mul]

theorem ejaLm_smul (r : ℝ) (a : V) : ejaLm (r • a) = r • ejaLm a := by
  ext w; simp [eja_smul_mul]

/-- `a ↦ L a` as a linear map. -/
def ejaLmL : V →ₗ[ℝ] (V →ₗ[ℝ] V) where
  toFun := ejaLm
  map_add' := ejaLm_add
  map_smul' := ejaLm_smul

/-- **The trace functional** `τ x = tr (L x)`. -/
def ejaTrL : V →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ := (LinearMap.trace ℝ V).comp ejaLmL

theorem ejaTrL_apply (a : V) : ejaTrL a = LinearMap.trace ℝ V (ejaLm a) := rfl

/-- The derivation identity, applied to a vector: this is what makes the trace
form associative. -/
theorem eja_der' (a b c d : V) :
    (a * (b * c) - b * (a * c)) * d
      = a * (b * (c * d)) - b * (a * (c * d)) - (c * (a * (b * d)) - c * (b * (a * d))) := by
  have h := eja_der a b c d
  simp only [eja_sub_mul]
  simp only [eja_mul_comm] at h ⊢
  linear_combination (norm := module) -h

/-- **The trace kills the commutator of two multiplications**: `τ (a * (b * c))`
is symmetric in `a` and `b`.  This is the associativity of the trace form. -/
theorem eja_tr_comm (a b c : V) : ejaTrL (a * (b * c)) = ejaTrL (b * (a * c)) := by
  have hL : ejaLm (a * (b * c) - b * (a * c))
      = (ejaLm a * ejaLm b - ejaLm b * ejaLm a) * ejaLm c
        - ejaLm c * (ejaLm a * ejaLm b - ejaLm b * ejaLm a) := by
    ext d
    simp only [LinearMap.sub_apply, Module.End.mul_apply, ejaLm_apply, eja_mul_sub]
    exact eja_der' a b c d
  have h0 : ejaTrL (a * (b * c) - b * (a * c)) = 0 := by
    rw [ejaTrL_apply, hL]
    rw [map_sub, LinearMap.trace_mul_comm]
    simp
  rw [map_sub] at h0
  linarith

/-- **The trace form** `B a b = τ (a * b)`. -/
def ejaB (a b : V) : ℝ := ejaTrL (a * b)

theorem ejaB_symm (a b : V) : ejaB a b = ejaB b a := by
  rw [ejaB, ejaB, eja_mul_comm]

/-- **The trace form is associative**: `B (a * b) c = B b (a * c)`. -/
theorem ejaB_assoc (a b c : V) : ejaB (a * b) c = ejaB b (a * c) := by
  rw [ejaB, ejaB, eja_mul_comm (a * b) c, eja_tr_comm c a b, eja_mul_comm c b,
    eja_tr_comm b a c]

theorem ejaB_add_right (a b c : V) : ejaB a (b + c) = ejaB a b + ejaB a c := by
  simp [ejaB, eja_mul_add, map_add]

theorem ejaB_sub_right (a b c : V) : ejaB a (b - c) = ejaB a b - ejaB a c := by
  simp [ejaB, eja_mul_sub, map_sub]

theorem ejaB_smul_right (r : ℝ) (a b : V) : ejaB a (r • b) = r * ejaB a b := by
  simp [ejaB, eja_mul_smul, map_smul, smul_eq_mul]

theorem ejaB_add_left (a b c : V) : ejaB (a + b) c = ejaB a c + ejaB b c := by
  rw [ejaB_symm, ejaB_add_right, ejaB_symm a c, ejaB_symm b c]

theorem ejaB_smul_left (r : ℝ) (a b : V) : ejaB (r • a) b = r * ejaB a b := by
  rw [ejaB_symm, ejaB_smul_right, ejaB_symm]

/-! ### The Peirce projections of an idempotent -/

/-- For an idempotent `p`, `2 L p ^ 3 - 3 L p ^ 2 + L p = 0`: this is the
Peirce relation, and it comes from the linearised Jordan identity at
`(p, p, y; p)`. -/
theorem eja_idem_cubic (p : V) (hp : p * p = p) (y : V) :
    p * (p * (p * y)) + p * (p * (p * y)) + p * y
      = p * (p * y) + p * (p * y) + p * (p * y) := by
  have h := eja_lin p p y p
  simp only [hp, eja_mul_comm] at h ⊢
  linear_combination (norm := module) h

/-- The projection onto the Peirce `1`-space of an idempotent `p`. -/
def ejaPone (p : V) : V →ₗ[ℝ] V := (2 : ℝ) • (ejaLm p * ejaLm p) - ejaLm p

/-- The projection onto the Peirce `½`-space of an idempotent `p`. -/
def ejaPhalf (p : V) : V →ₗ[ℝ] V := (4 : ℝ) • ejaLm p - (4 : ℝ) • (ejaLm p * ejaLm p)

theorem ejaPone_apply (p y : V) : ejaPone p y = (2 : ℝ) • (p * (p * y)) - p * y := rfl

theorem ejaPhalf_apply (p y : V) :
    ejaPhalf p y = (4 : ℝ) • (p * y) - (4 : ℝ) • (p * (p * y)) := rfl

theorem ejaPone_idem (p : V) (hp : p * p = p) : ejaPone p * ejaPone p = ejaPone p := by
  ext y
  have h1 := eja_idem_cubic p hp y
  have h2 := eja_idem_cubic p hp (p * y)
  simp only [Module.End.mul_apply, ejaPone_apply, eja_mul_sub, eja_mul_smul, smul_sub]
  linear_combination (norm := module) h1 + (2 : ℝ) • h2

theorem ejaPhalf_idem (p : V) (hp : p * p = p) : ejaPhalf p * ejaPhalf p = ejaPhalf p := by
  ext y
  have h1 := eja_idem_cubic p hp y
  have h2 := eja_idem_cubic p hp (p * y)
  simp only [Module.End.mul_apply, ejaPhalf_apply, eja_mul_sub, eja_mul_smul, smul_sub]
  linear_combination (norm := module) (8 : ℝ) • h2 - (4 : ℝ) • h1

theorem ejaLm_eq_peirce (p : V) :
    (2 : ℝ) • ejaLm p = (2 : ℝ) • ejaPone p + ejaPhalf p := by
  ext y
  simp only [LinearMap.smul_apply, LinearMap.add_apply, ejaPone_apply, ejaPhalf_apply,
    ejaLm_apply]
  module

theorem eja_trace_idem_nonneg (f : V →ₗ[ℝ] V) (hf : f * f = f) :
    0 ≤ LinearMap.trace ℝ V f := by
  rw [(LinearMap.IsIdempotentElem.isProj_range f hf).trace]
  exact Nat.cast_nonneg _

theorem eja_trace_idem_pos (f : V →ₗ[ℝ] V) (hf : f * f = f) (hne : f ≠ 0) :
    0 < LinearMap.trace ℝ V f := by
  refine lt_of_le_of_ne (eja_trace_idem_nonneg f hf) ?_
  intro hcon
  exact hne (LinearMap.IsIdempotentElem.eq_zero_of_trace_eq_zero hf hcon.symm)

/-- **The trace of a non-zero idempotent is positive.**  `L p` is the sum of the
projection onto the Peirce `1`-space and half the projection onto the Peirce
`½`-space; the first is non-zero because it fixes `p`. -/
theorem eja_tr_idem_pos (p : V) (hp : p * p = p) (hne : p ≠ 0) : 0 < ejaTrL p := by
  have hone : ejaPone p ≠ 0 := by
    intro hcon
    have : ejaPone p p = 0 := by rw [hcon]; rfl
    rw [ejaPone_apply, hp, hp] at this
    -- (2:ℝ) • p - p = p
    apply hne
    have h2 : (2 : ℝ) • p - p = p := by module
    rwa [h2] at this
  have h1 : 0 < LinearMap.trace ℝ V (ejaPone p) :=
    eja_trace_idem_pos _ (ejaPone_idem p hp) hone
  have h2 : 0 ≤ LinearMap.trace ℝ V (ejaPhalf p) :=
    eja_trace_idem_nonneg _ (ejaPhalf_idem p hp)
  have h3 : (2 : ℝ) * ejaTrL p
      = 2 * LinearMap.trace ℝ V (ejaPone p) + LinearMap.trace ℝ V (ejaPhalf p) := by
    have := congrArg (LinearMap.trace ℝ V) (ejaLm_eq_peirce p)
    rw [map_add, map_smul, map_smul] at this
    simpa [ejaTrL_apply, smul_eq_mul] using this
  linarith

/-! ### Evaluating polynomials at a single element -/

open Polynomial in
/-- Evaluation of a real polynomial at `x`, as a plain function. -/
def ejaEvF (x : V) (q : ℝ[X]) : V := q.sum fun n a => a • ejaPow x n

open Polynomial in
omit [PartialOrder V] [EuclideanJordanAlgebra V] in
theorem ejaEvF_monomial (x : V) (n : ℕ) (a : ℝ) :
    ejaEvF x (Polynomial.monomial n a) = a • ejaPow x n :=
  Polynomial.sum_monomial_index a _ (zero_smul ℝ _)

open Polynomial in
omit [PartialOrder V] [EuclideanJordanAlgebra V] in
theorem ejaEvF_add (x : V) (p q : ℝ[X]) :
    ejaEvF x (p + q) = ejaEvF x p + ejaEvF x q :=
  Polynomial.sum_add_index p q _ (fun _ => zero_smul ℝ _) (fun _ a b => add_smul a b _)

open Polynomial in
omit [PartialOrder V] [EuclideanJordanAlgebra V] in
theorem ejaEvF_smul (x : V) (r : ℝ) (q : ℝ[X]) :
    ejaEvF x (r • q) = r • ejaEvF x q := by
  induction q using Polynomial.induction_on' with
  | add p q hp hq => rw [smul_add, ejaEvF_add, ejaEvF_add, hp, hq, smul_add]
  | monomial n a =>
    rw [Polynomial.smul_monomial, ejaEvF_monomial, ejaEvF_monomial, smul_smul, smul_eq_mul]

open Polynomial in
/-- **Evaluation at `x`**, as a linear map `ℝ[X] →ₗ[ℝ] V`.  By
power-associativity it is multiplicative (`ejaEv_mul`), so its image is the
associative subalgebra generated by `x`. -/
def ejaEv (x : V) : ℝ[X] →ₗ[ℝ] V where
  toFun := ejaEvF x
  map_add' := ejaEvF_add x
  map_smul' r q := ejaEvF_smul x r q

open Polynomial in
omit [PartialOrder V] [EuclideanJordanAlgebra V] in
@[simp] theorem ejaEv_monomial (x : V) (n : ℕ) (a : ℝ) :
    ejaEv x (Polynomial.monomial n a) = a • ejaPow x n := ejaEvF_monomial x n a

open Polynomial in
/-- **Evaluation is multiplicative**; this is exactly power-associativity. -/
theorem ejaEv_mul (x : V) (p q : ℝ[X]) : ejaEv x (p * q) = ejaEv x p * ejaEv x q := by
  induction p using Polynomial.induction_on' with
  | add p₁ p₂ h₁ h₂ => rw [add_mul, map_add, map_add, h₁, h₂, eja_add_mul]
  | monomial n a =>
    induction q using Polynomial.induction_on' with
    | add q₁ q₂ h₁ h₂ => rw [mul_add, map_add, map_add, h₁, h₂, eja_mul_add]
    | monomial m b =>
      rw [Polynomial.monomial_mul_monomial, ejaEv_monomial, ejaEv_monomial,
        ejaEv_monomial, eja_smul_mul, eja_mul_smul, smul_smul, eja_pow_mul_pow]

open Polynomial in
omit [PartialOrder V] [EuclideanJordanAlgebra V] in
theorem ejaEv_one (x : V) : ejaEv x 1 = 1 := by
  have h : (Polynomial.monomial 0 (1 : ℝ)) = 1 := by
    rw [Polynomial.monomial_zero_left, map_one]
  rw [← h, ejaEv_monomial, ejaPow_zero, one_smul]

open Polynomial in
theorem ejaEv_X (x : V) : ejaEv x Polynomial.X = x := by
  rw [← Polynomial.monomial_one_one_eq_X, ejaEv_monomial, ejaPow_one, one_smul]

open Polynomial in
omit [PartialOrder V] [EuclideanJordanAlgebra V] in
theorem ejaEv_C (x : V) (r : ℝ) : ejaEv x (Polynomial.C r) = r • 1 := by
  rw [← Polynomial.monomial_zero_left, ejaEv_monomial, ejaPow_zero]

/-! ### The minimal polynomial -/

open Polynomial in
theorem eja_exists_ann (x : V) : ∃ q : ℝ[X], q ≠ 0 ∧ ejaEv x q = 0 := by
  classical
  set n := Module.finrank ℝ V with hn
  have hdep : ¬ LinearIndependent ℝ (fun i : Fin (n + 1) => ejaPow x (i : ℕ)) := by
    intro hli
    have := hli.fintype_card_le_finrank
    simp [hn] at this
  rw [Fintype.not_linearIndependent_iff] at hdep
  obtain ⟨g, hg, i, hi⟩ := hdep
  refine ⟨∑ j : Fin (n + 1), Polynomial.monomial (j : ℕ) (g j), ?_, ?_⟩
  · intro hcon
    have : (∑ j : Fin (n + 1), Polynomial.monomial (j : ℕ) (g j)).coeff (i : ℕ) = 0 := by
      rw [hcon]; simp
    rw [Polynomial.finsetSum_coeff] at this
    rw [Finset.sum_eq_single i] at this
    · simp at this
      exact hi this
    · intro b _ hb
      simp [Polynomial.coeff_monomial, Fin.val_inj, hb]
    · intro h; exact absurd (Finset.mem_univ i) h
  · rw [map_sum]
    simpa using hg

open Polynomial in
/-- **The minimal polynomial of `x`**: a monic polynomial killing `x` and
dividing every polynomial that kills `x`. -/
theorem eja_exists_minpoly (x : V) :
    ∃ q : ℝ[X], q.Monic ∧ ejaEv x q = 0 ∧ ∀ r : ℝ[X], ejaEv x r = 0 → q ∣ r := by
  classical
  obtain ⟨q0, hq0ne, hq0⟩ := eja_exists_ann x
  have hP : ∃ n : ℕ, ∃ q : ℝ[X], q ≠ 0 ∧ q.natDegree = n ∧ ejaEv x q = 0 :=
    ⟨q0.natDegree, q0, hq0ne, rfl, hq0⟩
  obtain ⟨q, hqne, hqd, hq0'⟩ := Nat.find_spec hP
  have hlc : q.leadingCoeff ≠ 0 := Polynomial.leadingCoeff_ne_zero.mpr hqne
  set q1 : ℝ[X] := q * Polynomial.C q.leadingCoeff⁻¹ with hq1
  have hq1m : q1.Monic := Polynomial.monic_mul_leadingCoeff_inv hqne
  have hq1z : ejaEv x q1 = 0 := by rw [hq1, ejaEv_mul, hq0', eja_zero_mul]
  have hq1d : q1.natDegree = q.natDegree := by
    rw [hq1, Polynomial.natDegree_mul_C (inv_ne_zero hlc)]
  refine ⟨q1, hq1m, hq1z, ?_⟩
  intro r hr
  rw [← Polynomial.modByMonic_eq_zero_iff_dvd hq1m]
  by_contra hcon
  have hz : ejaEv x (r %ₘ q1) = 0 := by
    have h := Polynomial.modByMonic_add_div r q1
    have h2 : ejaEv x (r %ₘ q1) + ejaEv x q1 * ejaEv x (r /ₘ q1) = ejaEv x r := by
      rw [← ejaEv_mul, ← map_add, h]
    rw [hq1z, eja_zero_mul, add_zero, hr] at h2
    exact h2
  have hdeg : (r %ₘ q1).natDegree < q1.natDegree :=
    Polynomial.natDegree_lt_natDegree hcon (Polynomial.degree_modByMonic_lt r hq1m)
  have hmin := Nat.find_min' hP ⟨r %ₘ q1, hcon, rfl, hz⟩
  omega

/-! ### The minimal polynomial splits into distinct linear factors -/

open Polynomial in
/-- A polynomial vanishing on a finite set of reals is divisible by the
corresponding product of linear factors. -/
theorem eja_prod_X_sub_C_dvd (s : Finset ℝ) :
    ∀ p : ℝ[X], (∀ l ∈ s, p.eval l = 0) →
      (∏ l ∈ s, (Polynomial.X - Polynomial.C l)) ∣ p := by
  classical
  refine Finset.induction_on s ?_ ?_
  · intro p _; simp
  · intro a t ha ih p h
    obtain ⟨g, hg⟩ : (Polynomial.X - Polynomial.C a) ∣ p :=
      Polynomial.dvd_iff_isRoot.mpr (h a (Finset.mem_insert_self a t))
    have hgz : ∀ l ∈ t, g.eval l = 0 := by
      intro l hl
      have h0 : p.eval l = 0 := h l (Finset.mem_insert_of_mem hl)
      rw [hg] at h0
      simp only [Polynomial.eval_mul, Polynomial.eval_sub, Polynomial.eval_X,
        Polynomial.eval_C] at h0
      have hne : l - a ≠ 0 := sub_ne_zero.mpr (by rintro rfl; exact ha hl)
      exact (mul_eq_zero.mp h0).resolve_left hne
    obtain ⟨w, hw⟩ := ih g hgz
    refine ⟨w, ?_⟩
    rw [Finset.prod_insert ha, hg, hw]
    ring

open Polynomial in
/-- **The minimal polynomial splits over `ℝ`**: it is a product of linear
factors.  A monic quadratic factor with negative discriminant is
`(X + b/2)² + d` with `d > 0`, and multiplying the minimal polynomial by the
complementary factor exhibits `0` as a sum of two squares, which formal reality
forbids. -/
theorem eja_minpoly_split (x : V) {m : ℝ[X]} (hmm : m.Monic) (hm0 : ejaEv x m = 0)
    (hdvd : ∀ r : ℝ[X], ejaEv x r = 0 → m ∣ r) :
    ∀ n : ℕ, ∀ q : ℝ[X], q.natDegree = n → q.Monic → q ∣ m →
      ∃ l : Multiset ℝ, q = (l.map fun a => Polynomial.X - Polynomial.C a).prod := by
  intro n
  induction n using Nat.strong_induction_on with
  | _ n ih =>
    intro q hqd hqm hqdvd
    rcases Nat.eq_zero_or_pos n with rfl | hn
    · refine ⟨0, ?_⟩
      simp only [Multiset.map_zero, Multiset.prod_zero]
      have h1 : q = Polynomial.C (q.coeff 0) := Polynomial.eq_C_of_natDegree_eq_zero hqd
      have h2 : q.coeff 0 = 1 := by
        have h3 := hqm.leadingCoeff
        rwa [Polynomial.leadingCoeff, hqd] at h3
      rw [h1, h2, map_one]
    · obtain ⟨k, rfl⟩ : ∃ k, n = k + 1 := ⟨n - 1, by omega⟩
      have hq : Polynomial.IsMonicOfDegree q (k + 1) := ⟨hqd, hqm⟩
      obtain ⟨f₁, f₂, hf₁, hfe⟩ := hq.eq_isMonicOfDegree_one_or_two_mul
      have hf₂dvd : f₂ ∣ m := dvd_trans ⟨f₁, by rw [hfe]; ring⟩ hqdvd
      rcases hf₁ with hd1 | hd2
      · -- a linear factor
        obtain ⟨r, hr⟩ := Polynomial.isMonicOfDegree_one_iff.mp hd1
        have hq' : Polynomial.IsMonicOfDegree (f₁ * f₂) (1 + k) := by
          rw [← hfe, add_comm]; exact hq
        have hf₂ : Polynomial.IsMonicOfDegree f₂ k := hd1.of_mul_left hq'
        obtain ⟨l, hl⟩ := ih k (by omega) f₂ hf₂.natDegree_eq hf₂.monic hf₂dvd
        refine ⟨(-r) ::ₘ l, ?_⟩
        rw [Multiset.map_cons, Multiset.prod_cons, ← hl, hfe, hr]
        simp
      · -- a quadratic factor: its discriminant is non-negative
        obtain ⟨a, b, hab⟩ := Polynomial.isMonicOfDegree_two_iff.mp hd2
        have hf₁dvd : f₁ ∣ m := dvd_trans ⟨f₂, hfe⟩ hqdvd
        have hdisc : 0 ≤ a ^ 2 - 4 * b := by
          by_contra hcon
          rw [not_le] at hcon
          set c : ℝ := b - a ^ 2 / 4 with hc
          have hcpos : 0 < c := by rw [hc]; linarith
          obtain ⟨t, ht⟩ := hf₁dvd
          have hsplit : f₁ = (Polynomial.X + Polynomial.C (a / 2)) ^ 2 + Polynomial.C c := by
            rw [hab, hc]
            rw [show (Polynomial.C (b - a ^ 2 / 4) : ℝ[X])
                = Polynomial.C b - Polynomial.C (a ^ 2 / 4) by rw [map_sub]]
            have hcc : (Polynomial.C (a ^ 2 / 4) : ℝ[X])
                = Polynomial.C (a / 2) * Polynomial.C (a / 2) := by
              rw [← map_mul]; congr 1; ring
            have hca : (Polynomial.C a : ℝ[X])
                = Polynomial.C (a / 2) + Polynomial.C (a / 2) := by
              rw [← map_add]; congr 1; ring
            rw [hcc, hca]
            ring
          have hkey : m * t
              = ((Polynomial.X + Polynomial.C (a / 2)) * t) ^ 2 + Polynomial.C c * t ^ 2 := by
            conv_lhs => rw [ht, hsplit]
            ring
          have h0 : ejaEv x (m * t) = 0 := by rw [ejaEv_mul, hm0, eja_zero_mul]
          rw [hkey] at h0
          set u := ejaEv x ((Polynomial.X + Polynomial.C (a / 2)) * t) with hu
          set v := ejaEv x t with hv
          have h1 : ejaEv x (((Polynomial.X + Polynomial.C (a / 2)) * t) ^ 2) = u * u := by
            rw [sq, ejaEv_mul]
          have h2 : ejaEv x (Polynomial.C c * t ^ 2) = c • (v * v) := by
            rw [ejaEv_mul, sq, ejaEv_mul, ejaEv_C, eja_smul_mul, eja_one_mul]
          rw [map_add, h1, h2] at h0
          have hsq : c • (v * v) = (Real.sqrt c • v) * (Real.sqrt c • v) := by
            rw [eja_smul_mul, eja_mul_smul, smul_smul, Real.mul_self_sqrt hcpos.le]
          rw [hsq] at h0
          have hu0 : u = 0 :=
            EuclideanJordanAlgebra.formallyReal (IsSumSq.mul_self _) h0
          have h0' : (Real.sqrt c • v) * (Real.sqrt c • v) = 0 := by
            rw [hu0] at h0
            have hz : (0 : V) * (0 : V) = 0 := eja_zero_mul 0
            rw [hz, zero_add] at h0
            exact h0
          have hv0 : Real.sqrt c • v = 0 :=
            EuclideanJordanAlgebra.formallyReal IsSumSq.zero (by rw [add_zero]; exact h0')
          have hvz : v = 0 := by
            have hs : Real.sqrt c ≠ 0 := ne_of_gt (Real.sqrt_pos.mpr hcpos)
            exact (smul_eq_zero.mp hv0).resolve_left hs
          have hmt : m ∣ t := hdvd t hvz
          have htne : t ≠ 0 := by
            intro hcon2
            rw [hcon2, mul_zero] at ht
            exact hmm.ne_zero ht
          have hdle : m.natDegree ≤ t.natDegree := Polynomial.natDegree_le_of_dvd hmt htne
          have hf1ne : f₁ ≠ 0 := hd2.ne_zero
          have hmd : m.natDegree = 2 + t.natDegree := by
            rw [ht, Polynomial.natDegree_mul hf1ne htne, hd2.natDegree_eq]
          omega
        -- factor the quadratic
        set r₁ : ℝ := (-a + Real.sqrt (a ^ 2 - 4 * b)) / 2 with hr₁
        set r₂ : ℝ := (-a - Real.sqrt (a ^ 2 - 4 * b)) / 2 with hr₂
        have hts : Real.sqrt (a ^ 2 - 4 * b) * Real.sqrt (a ^ 2 - 4 * b) = a ^ 2 - 4 * b :=
          Real.mul_self_sqrt hdisc
        have hsum : r₁ + r₂ = -a := by rw [hr₁, hr₂]; ring
        have hprod : r₁ * r₂ = b := by
          rw [hr₁, hr₂]
          linear_combination (-1/4 : ℝ) * hts
        have hfact : f₁ = (Polynomial.X - Polynomial.C r₁) * (Polynomial.X - Polynomial.C r₂) := by
          have e : (Polynomial.X - Polynomial.C r₁) * (Polynomial.X - Polynomial.C r₂)
              = Polynomial.X ^ 2 - Polynomial.C (r₁ + r₂) * Polynomial.X
                + Polynomial.C (r₁ * r₂) := by
            rw [map_add, map_mul]; ring
          rw [e, hsum, hprod, hab, map_neg]
          ring
        have hf₂ne : f₂ ≠ 0 := by
          intro hcon
          rw [hcon, mul_zero] at hfe
          exact hqm.ne_zero hfe
        have hdegq : k + 1 = 2 + f₂.natDegree := by
          have h5 := hq.natDegree_eq
          rw [hfe, Polynomial.natDegree_mul hd2.ne_zero hf₂ne, hd2.natDegree_eq] at h5
          omega
        have hq' : Polynomial.IsMonicOfDegree (f₁ * f₂) (2 + (k - 1)) := by
          rw [← hfe, show 2 + (k - 1) = k + 1 by omega]
          exact hq
        have hf₂ : Polynomial.IsMonicOfDegree f₂ (k - 1) := hd2.of_mul_left hq'
        obtain ⟨l, hl⟩ := ih (k - 1) (by omega) f₂ hf₂.natDegree_eq hf₂.monic hf₂dvd
        refine ⟨r₁ ::ₘ r₂ ::ₘ l, ?_⟩
        rw [Multiset.map_cons, Multiset.prod_cons, Multiset.map_cons, Multiset.prod_cons,
          ← hl, hfe, hfact]
        ring

open Polynomial in
/-- **The minimal polynomial is squarefree**: a repeated linear factor would
make a non-zero element of the subalgebra square to zero. -/
theorem eja_minpoly_nodup (x : V) {m : ℝ[X]} (hmm : m.Monic) (hm0 : ejaEv x m = 0)
    (hdvd : ∀ r : ℝ[X], ejaEv x r = 0 → m ∣ r)
    {l : Multiset ℝ} (hl : m = (l.map fun a => Polynomial.X - Polynomial.C a).prod) :
    l.Nodup := by
  classical
  by_contra hcon
  simp only [Multiset.nodup_iff_count_le_one, not_forall, not_le] at hcon
  obtain ⟨a, ha⟩ := hcon
  have hle : Multiset.replicate 2 a ≤ l := Multiset.le_count_iff_replicate_le.mp ha
  have hdvd2 : (Polynomial.X - Polynomial.C a) * (Polynomial.X - Polynomial.C a) ∣ m := by
    rw [hl]
    have h1 := Multiset.prod_dvd_prod_of_le
      (Multiset.map_le_map (f := fun a : ℝ => Polynomial.X - Polynomial.C a) hle)
    simpa [Multiset.map_replicate, Multiset.prod_replicate, sq] using h1
  obtain ⟨t, ht⟩ := hdvd2
  have hxne : (Polynomial.X - Polynomial.C a : ℝ[X]) ≠ 0 := Polynomial.X_sub_C_ne_zero a
  have htne : t ≠ 0 := by
    intro hcon2; rw [hcon2, mul_zero] at ht; exact hmm.ne_zero ht
  have hkey : ((Polynomial.X - Polynomial.C a) * t) * ((Polynomial.X - Polynomial.C a) * t)
      = m * t := by rw [ht]; ring
  have h0 : ejaEv x ((Polynomial.X - Polynomial.C a) * t)
      * ejaEv x ((Polynomial.X - Polynomial.C a) * t) = 0 := by
    rw [← ejaEv_mul, hkey, ejaEv_mul, hm0, eja_zero_mul]
  have hu0 : ejaEv x ((Polynomial.X - Polynomial.C a) * t) = 0 :=
    EuclideanJordanAlgebra.formallyReal IsSumSq.zero (by rw [add_zero]; exact h0)
  have hmu : m ∣ (Polynomial.X - Polynomial.C a) * t := hdvd _ hu0
  have hune : (Polynomial.X - Polynomial.C a) * t ≠ 0 := mul_ne_zero hxne htne
  have h1 : m.natDegree ≤ ((Polynomial.X - Polynomial.C a) * t).natDegree :=
    Polynomial.natDegree_le_of_dvd hmu hune
  have h2 : m.natDegree = 2 + t.natDegree := by
    rw [ht, Polynomial.natDegree_mul (mul_ne_zero hxne hxne) htne,
      Polynomial.natDegree_mul hxne hxne]
    simp
  have h3 : ((Polynomial.X - Polynomial.C a) * t).natDegree = 1 + t.natDegree := by
    rw [Polynomial.natDegree_mul hxne htne]
    simp
  omega

open Polynomial in
/-- **The spectral theorem for a single element** of a Euclidean Jordan
algebra: every `x` is a real linear combination of finitely many pairwise
orthogonal non-zero idempotents summing to `1`.  The idempotents are the
Lagrange basis polynomials of the roots of the minimal polynomial, evaluated
at `x`. -/
theorem eja_spectral (x : V) :
    ∃ (s : Finset ℝ) (e : ℝ → V),
      (∀ l ∈ s, e l * e l = e l) ∧
      (∀ l ∈ s, ∀ k ∈ s, l ≠ k → e l * e k = 0) ∧
      (∀ l ∈ s, e l ≠ 0) ∧
      (∑ l ∈ s, e l = 1) ∧
      x = ∑ l ∈ s, l • e l := by
  classical
  obtain ⟨m, hmm, hm0, hdvd⟩ := eja_exists_minpoly x
  obtain ⟨lm, hlm⟩ :=
    eja_minpoly_split x hmm hm0 hdvd m.natDegree m rfl hmm dvd_rfl
  have hnd : lm.Nodup := eja_minpoly_nodup x hmm hm0 hdvd hlm
  set s : Finset ℝ := ⟨lm, hnd⟩ with hs
  set e : ℝ → V := fun l => ejaEv x (Lagrange.basis s id l) with he
  have hel : ∀ l : ℝ, e l = ejaEv x (Lagrange.basis s id l) := fun _ => rfl
  have hm : m = ∏ k ∈ s, (Polynomial.X - Polynomial.C k) := by rw [hlm]; rfl
  have hinj : Set.InjOn id (s : Set ℝ) := fun _ _ _ _ h => h
  have hzero : ∀ p : ℝ[X], (∀ k ∈ s, p.eval k = 0) → ejaEv x p = 0 := by
    intro p hp
    obtain ⟨w, hw⟩ : m ∣ p := by rw [hm]; exact eja_prod_X_sub_C_dvd s p hp
    rw [hw, ejaEv_mul, hm0, eja_zero_mul]
  have hbself : ∀ l ∈ s, Polynomial.eval l (Lagrange.basis s (id : ℝ → ℝ) l) = 1 :=
    fun l hl => Lagrange.eval_basis_self hinj hl
  have hbne : ∀ l k : ℝ, k ∈ s → l ≠ k →
      Polynomial.eval k (Lagrange.basis s (id : ℝ → ℝ) l) = 0 :=
    fun _ _ hk hlk => Lagrange.eval_basis_of_ne (v := (id : ℝ → ℝ)) hlk hk
  have hbev : ∀ l ∈ s, ∀ k ∈ s,
      Polynomial.eval k (Lagrange.basis s (id : ℝ → ℝ) l) = if l = k then (1 : ℝ) else 0 := by
    intro l hl k hk
    by_cases hlk : l = k
    · subst hlk
      simpa using hbself l hl
    · simpa [hlk] using hbne l k hk hlk
  have hidem : ∀ l ∈ s, e l * e l = e l := by
    intro l hl
    have h := hzero (Lagrange.basis s id l * Lagrange.basis s id l - Lagrange.basis s id l) ?_
    · rw [map_sub, ejaEv_mul] at h
      rw [hel]
      exact sub_eq_zero.mp h
    · intro k hk
      simp only [Polynomial.eval_sub, Polynomial.eval_mul, hbev l hl k hk]
      by_cases hlk : l = k <;> simp [hlk]
  have horth : ∀ l ∈ s, ∀ k ∈ s, l ≠ k → e l * e k = 0 := by
    intro l hl k hk hlk
    have h := hzero (Lagrange.basis s id l * Lagrange.basis s id k) ?_
    · rw [ejaEv_mul] at h
      rw [hel, hel]
      exact h
    · intro j hj
      simp only [Polynomial.eval_mul, hbev l hl j hj, hbev k hk j hj]
      by_cases h1 : l = j
      · have h2 : k ≠ j := by rintro rfl; exact hlk h1
        simp [h1, h2]
      · simp [h1]
  have hcard : m.natDegree = s.card := by
    rw [hm, show (∏ k ∈ s, (Polynomial.X - Polynomial.C k)) = Lagrange.nodal s id from rfl,
      Lagrange.natDegree_nodal]
  have hne0 : ∀ l ∈ s, e l ≠ 0 := by
    intro l hl hcon
    rw [hel] at hcon
    have hdd : m ∣ Lagrange.basis s id l := hdvd _ hcon
    have hbne : Lagrange.basis s id l ≠ 0 := Lagrange.basis_ne_zero hinj hl
    have h1 : m.natDegree ≤ (Lagrange.basis s id l).natDegree :=
      Polynomial.natDegree_le_of_dvd hdd hbne
    rw [hcard, Lagrange.natDegree_basis hinj hl] at h1
    have h2 : 0 < s.card := Finset.card_pos.mpr ⟨l, hl⟩
    omega
  have hsum : ∑ l ∈ s, e l = 1 := by
    rcases Finset.eq_empty_or_nonempty s with hemp | hsne
    · have h1 : m = 1 := by rw [hm, hemp, Finset.prod_empty]
      have h2 : (1 : V) = 0 := by rw [← ejaEv_one x, ← h1, hm0]
      rw [hemp, Finset.sum_empty, h2]
    · have h := hzero ((∑ l ∈ s, Lagrange.basis s id l) - 1) ?_
      · rw [map_sub, map_sum, ejaEv_one] at h
        rw [Finset.sum_congr rfl (fun l _ => hel l)]
        exact sub_eq_zero.mp h
      · intro k hk
        simp only [Polynomial.eval_sub, Polynomial.eval_finsetSum, Polynomial.eval_one]
        rw [Finset.sum_congr rfl (fun l hl => hbev l hl k hk)]
        simp [Finset.sum_ite_eq', hk]
  have hx : x = ∑ l ∈ s, l • e l := by
    rcases Finset.eq_empty_or_nonempty s with hemp | hsne
    · have h2 : ejaEv x Polynomial.X = 0 := by
        refine hzero _ ?_
        intro k hk
        rw [hemp] at hk
        exact absurd hk (Finset.notMem_empty k)
      rw [ejaEv_X] at h2
      rw [hemp, Finset.sum_empty, h2]
    · have h := hzero (Polynomial.X - ∑ l ∈ s, Polynomial.C l * Lagrange.basis s id l) ?_
      · rw [map_sub, map_sum, ejaEv_X] at h
        have h2 : ∀ l ∈ s, ejaEv x (Polynomial.C l * Lagrange.basis s id l) = l • e l := by
          intro l _
          rw [ejaEv_mul, ejaEv_C, eja_smul_mul, eja_one_mul, hel]
        rw [Finset.sum_congr rfl h2] at h
        exact sub_eq_zero.mp h
      · intro k hk
        simp only [Polynomial.eval_sub, Polynomial.eval_X, Polynomial.eval_finsetSum,
          Polynomial.eval_mul, Polynomial.eval_C]
        rw [Finset.sum_congr rfl (fun l hl => by rw [hbev l hl k hk])]
        simp [mul_ite, Finset.sum_ite_eq', hk]
  exact ⟨s, e, hidem, horth, hne0, hsum, hx⟩

/-! ### The cone of squares, and the spectral characterisation of the order -/

theorem eja_sum_mul {ι : Type*} (s : Finset ι) (f : ι → V) (b : V) :
    (∑ i ∈ s, f i) * b = ∑ i ∈ s, f i * b := by
  classical
  refine Finset.induction_on s ?_ ?_
  · simp [eja_zero_mul]
  · intro a t ha ih
    rw [Finset.sum_insert ha, Finset.sum_insert ha, eja_add_mul, ih]

theorem eja_mul_sum {ι : Type*} (b : V) (s : Finset ι) (f : ι → V) :
    b * (∑ i ∈ s, f i) = ∑ i ∈ s, b * f i := by
  classical
  refine Finset.induction_on s ?_ ?_
  · simp [eja_mul_zero]
  · intro a t ha ih
    rw [Finset.sum_insert ha, Finset.sum_insert ha, eja_mul_add, ih]

/-- Multiplying two combinations of the same orthogonal family of idempotents
multiplies the coefficients. -/
theorem eja_ortho_mul {s : Finset ℝ} {e : ℝ → V}
    (hidem : ∀ l ∈ s, e l * e l = e l)
    (horth : ∀ l ∈ s, ∀ k ∈ s, l ≠ k → e l * e k = 0) (c d : ℝ → ℝ) :
    (∑ l ∈ s, c l • e l) * (∑ k ∈ s, d k • e k) = ∑ l ∈ s, (c l * d l) • e l := by
  classical
  rw [eja_sum_mul]
  refine Finset.sum_congr rfl ?_
  intro l hl
  rw [eja_mul_sum, Finset.sum_eq_single l]
  · rw [eja_smul_mul, eja_mul_smul, smul_smul, hidem l hl]
  · intro k hk hkl
    rw [eja_smul_mul, eja_mul_smul, horth l hl k hk (Ne.symm hkl), smul_zero, smul_zero]
  · intro h; exact absurd hl h

theorem ejaB_sub_left (a b c : V) : ejaB (a - b) c = ejaB a c - ejaB b c := by
  rw [ejaB_symm, ejaB_sub_right, ejaB_symm a c, ejaB_symm b c]

/-- **The trace form is positive semi-definite**: by the spectral theorem,
`B a a = ∑ λ² τ(e λ)` with every `τ (e λ)` positive. -/
theorem ejaB_self_nonneg (a : V) : 0 ≤ ejaB a a := by
  obtain ⟨s, e, hidem, horth, hne0, hsum, hdec⟩ := eja_spectral a
  have h : a * a = ∑ l ∈ s, (l * l) • e l := by
    conv_lhs => rw [hdec]
    exact eja_ortho_mul hidem horth (fun l => l) (fun l => l)
  rw [ejaB, h, map_sum]
  refine Finset.sum_nonneg ?_
  intro l hl
  rw [map_smul, smul_eq_mul]
  exact mul_nonneg (mul_self_nonneg l) (eja_tr_idem_pos (e l) (hidem l hl) (hne0 l hl)).le

/-- **The trace form is positive definite.** -/
theorem ejaB_self_pos {a : V} (ha : a ≠ 0) : 0 < ejaB a a := by
  obtain ⟨s, e, hidem, horth, hne0, hsum, hdec⟩ := eja_spectral a
  have h : a * a = ∑ l ∈ s, (l * l) • e l := by
    conv_lhs => rw [hdec]
    exact eja_ortho_mul hidem horth (fun l => l) (fun l => l)
  have hw : ∃ l ∈ s, l ≠ 0 := by
    by_contra hcon
    simp only [not_exists, not_and, ne_eq, not_not] at hcon
    refine ha ?_
    rw [hdec]
    refine Finset.sum_eq_zero ?_
    intro l hl
    rw [hcon l hl, zero_smul]
  obtain ⟨l0, hl0, hl0ne⟩ := hw
  rw [ejaB, h, map_sum]
  refine Finset.sum_pos' ?_ ⟨l0, hl0, ?_⟩
  · intro l hl
    rw [map_smul, smul_eq_mul]
    exact mul_nonneg (mul_self_nonneg l) (eja_tr_idem_pos (e l) (hidem l hl) (hne0 l hl)).le
  · rw [map_smul, smul_eq_mul]
    have h1 : 0 < l0 * l0 := mul_self_pos.mpr hl0ne
    exact mul_pos h1 (eja_tr_idem_pos (e l0) (hidem l0 hl0) (hne0 l0 hl0))

theorem ejaPone_idem_apply (p : V) (hp : p * p = p) (a : V) :
    ejaPone p (ejaPone p a) = ejaPone p a := by
  have h := congrArg (fun f : V →ₗ[ℝ] V => f a) (ejaPone_idem p hp)
  simpa [Module.End.mul_apply] using h

theorem ejaPhalf_idem_apply (p : V) (hp : p * p = p) (a : V) :
    ejaPhalf p (ejaPhalf p a) = ejaPhalf p a := by
  have h := congrArg (fun f : V →ₗ[ℝ] V => f a) (ejaPhalf_idem p hp)
  simpa [Module.End.mul_apply] using h

theorem ejaB_pone_self_adj (p a b : V) :
    ejaB a (ejaPone p b) = ejaB (ejaPone p a) b := by
  rw [ejaPone_apply, ejaPone_apply, ejaB_sub_right, ejaB_smul_right, ejaB_sub_left,
    ejaB_smul_left, ← ejaB_assoc p a (p * b), ← ejaB_assoc p (p * a) b, ← ejaB_assoc p a b]

theorem ejaB_phalf_self_adj (p a b : V) :
    ejaB a (ejaPhalf p b) = ejaB (ejaPhalf p a) b := by
  rw [ejaPhalf_apply, ejaPhalf_apply, ejaB_sub_right, ejaB_smul_right, ejaB_smul_right,
    ejaB_sub_left, ejaB_smul_left, ejaB_smul_left, ← ejaB_assoc p a (p * b),
    ← ejaB_assoc p (p * a) b, ← ejaB_assoc p a b]

/-- `L p` is positive semi-definite for the trace form when `p` is an
idempotent: it is the sum of the projection onto the Peirce `1`-space and half
the projection onto the Peirce `½`-space, and both are self-adjoint
idempotents. -/
theorem ejaB_idem_apply_nonneg (p : V) (hp : p * p = p) (a : V) : 0 ≤ ejaB a (p * a) := by
  have h : (2 : ℝ) • (p * a) = (2 : ℝ) • ejaPone p a + ejaPhalf p a := by
    have hpe := congrArg (fun f : V →ₗ[ℝ] V => f a) (ejaLm_eq_peirce (V := V) p)
    simpa using hpe
  have h2 : (2 : ℝ) * ejaB a (p * a)
      = 2 * ejaB a (ejaPone p a) + ejaB a (ejaPhalf p a) := by
    rw [← ejaB_smul_right, h, ejaB_add_right, ejaB_smul_right]
  have h3 : ejaB a (ejaPone p a) = ejaB (ejaPone p a) (ejaPone p a) := by
    have h4 := ejaB_pone_self_adj p a (ejaPone p a)
    rwa [ejaPone_idem_apply p hp a] at h4
  have h5 : ejaB a (ejaPhalf p a) = ejaB (ejaPhalf p a) (ejaPhalf p a) := by
    have h6 := ejaB_phalf_self_adj p a (ejaPhalf p a)
    rwa [ejaPhalf_idem_apply p hp a] at h6
  have h7 : 0 ≤ ejaB (ejaPone p a) (ejaPone p a) := ejaB_self_nonneg _
  have h8 : 0 ≤ ejaB (ejaPhalf p a) (ejaPhalf p a) := ejaB_self_nonneg _
  rw [h3, h5] at h2
  linarith

theorem ejaB_sq_idem_nonneg (p : V) (hp : p * p = p) (a : V) : 0 ≤ ejaB (a * a) p := by
  rw [ejaB_assoc a a p, eja_mul_comm a p]
  exact ejaB_idem_apply_nonneg p hp a

/-- A sum of squares pairs non-negatively with every idempotent. -/
theorem eja_isSumSq_ejaB_idem_nonneg {z : V} (hz : IsSumSq z) (p : V) (hp : p * p = p) :
    0 ≤ ejaB z p := by
  induction hz with
  | zero => simp [ejaB, eja_zero_mul]
  | @sq_add a t _ ih =>
    rw [ejaB_add_left]
    exact add_nonneg (ejaB_sq_idem_nonneg p hp a) ih

/-- **The spectral criterion for positivity**: a combination of pairwise
orthogonal non-zero idempotents is a sum of squares exactly when all its
coefficients are non-negative. -/
theorem eja_ortho_isSumSq_iff {s : Finset ℝ} {e : ℝ → V}
    (hidem : ∀ l ∈ s, e l * e l = e l)
    (horth : ∀ l ∈ s, ∀ k ∈ s, l ≠ k → e l * e k = 0)
    (hne0 : ∀ l ∈ s, e l ≠ 0) (c : ℝ → ℝ) :
    IsSumSq (∑ l ∈ s, c l • e l) ↔ ∀ l ∈ s, 0 ≤ c l := by
  classical
  constructor
  · intro h l hl
    have hmul : (∑ k ∈ s, c k • e k) * e l = c l • e l := by
      rw [eja_sum_mul, Finset.sum_eq_single l]
      · rw [eja_smul_mul, hidem l hl]
      · intro k hk hkl
        rw [eja_smul_mul, horth k hk l hl hkl, smul_zero]
      · intro h'; exact absurd hl h'
    have hB : ejaB (∑ k ∈ s, c k • e k) (e l) = c l * ejaTrL (e l) := by
      rw [ejaB, hmul, map_smul, smul_eq_mul]
    have h0 : 0 ≤ ejaB (∑ k ∈ s, c k • e k) (e l) :=
      eja_isSumSq_ejaB_idem_nonneg h (e l) (hidem l hl)
    rw [hB] at h0
    have hpos : 0 < ejaTrL (e l) := eja_tr_idem_pos (e l) (hidem l hl) (hne0 l hl)
    by_contra hcon
    rw [not_le] at hcon
    nlinarith
  · intro h
    have hsq : (∑ l ∈ s, Real.sqrt (c l) • e l) * (∑ l ∈ s, Real.sqrt (c l) • e l)
        = ∑ l ∈ s, c l • e l := by
      rw [eja_ortho_mul hidem horth]
      refine Finset.sum_congr rfl ?_
      intro l hl
      rw [Real.mul_self_sqrt (h l hl)]
    rw [← hsq]
    exact IsSumSq.mul_self _

/-- **Every sum of squares is a square.**  This closes the gap the file's
header documents: the order cone of a Euclidean Jordan algebra, defined as the
cone of *sums* of squares, is the cone of squares. -/
theorem eja_isSumSq_iff_exists_sq (z : V) : IsSumSq z ↔ ∃ a : V, z = a * a := by
  constructor
  · intro hz
    obtain ⟨s, e, hidem, horth, hne0, hsum, hdec⟩ := eja_spectral z
    have hc : ∀ l ∈ s, 0 ≤ l := by
      refine (eja_ortho_isSumSq_iff hidem horth hne0 (fun l => l)).mp ?_
      rw [← hdec]; exact hz
    refine ⟨∑ l ∈ s, Real.sqrt l • e l, ?_⟩
    rw [eja_ortho_mul hidem horth]
    conv_lhs => rw [hdec]
    refine Finset.sum_congr rfl ?_
    intro l hl
    rw [Real.mul_self_sqrt (hc l hl)]
  · rintro ⟨a, rfl⟩
    exact IsSumSq.mul_self a

/-- **The order cone is the cone of squares.** -/
theorem eja_nonneg_iff_exists_sq (z : V) : 0 ≤ z ↔ ∃ a : V, z = a * a := by
  rw [eja_nonneg_iff]
  exact eja_isSumSq_iff_exists_sq z

theorem eja_le_iff_exists_sq (y z : V) : y ≤ z ↔ ∃ a : V, z - y = a * a := by
  rw [eja_le_iff]
  exact eja_isSumSq_iff_exists_sq (z - y)

/-- **An element is in the unit interval exactly when its spectral values
are.** -/
theorem eja_ortho_unit_interval_iff {s : Finset ℝ} {e : ℝ → V}
    (hidem : ∀ l ∈ s, e l * e l = e l)
    (horth : ∀ l ∈ s, ∀ k ∈ s, l ≠ k → e l * e k = 0)
    (hne0 : ∀ l ∈ s, e l ≠ 0) (hsum : ∑ l ∈ s, e l = 1) (c : ℝ → ℝ) :
    (0 ≤ (∑ l ∈ s, c l • e l) ∧ (∑ l ∈ s, c l • e l) ≤ 1)
      ↔ ∀ l ∈ s, 0 ≤ c l ∧ c l ≤ 1 := by
  have hone : (1 : V) - ∑ l ∈ s, c l • e l = ∑ l ∈ s, (1 - c l) • e l := by
    conv_lhs => rw [← hsum]
    rw [← Finset.sum_sub_distrib]
    refine Finset.sum_congr rfl ?_
    intro l _
    rw [sub_smul, one_smul]
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨h1, h2⟩ l hl
    rw [eja_nonneg_iff, eja_ortho_isSumSq_iff hidem horth hne0] at h1
    rw [eja_le_iff, hone, eja_ortho_isSumSq_iff hidem horth hne0] at h2
    exact ⟨h1 l hl, by linarith [h2 l hl]⟩
  · intro h
    constructor
    · rw [eja_nonneg_iff, eja_ortho_isSumSq_iff hidem horth hne0]
      exact fun l hl => (h l hl).1
    · rw [eja_le_iff, hone, eja_ortho_isSumSq_iff hidem horth hne0]
      intro l hl
      linarith [(h l hl).2]

/-! ### The states of a Euclidean Jordan algebra separate its points -/

/-- **For `x ≠ 0` there is a state not vanishing at `x`**: take a spectral
idempotent `p` of `x` with non-zero eigenvalue and the normalised trace-form
functional `y ↦ B y p / τ p`.  It is positive because a sum of squares pairs
non-negatively with an idempotent, and unital because `B 1 p = τ p`. -/
theorem eja_exists_state_ne_zero {x : V} (hx : x ≠ 0) :
    ∃ f : V →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ, (∀ y : V, 0 ≤ y → 0 ≤ f y) ∧ f 1 = 1 ∧ f x ≠ 0 := by
  classical
  obtain ⟨s, e, hidem, horth, hne0, hsum, hdec⟩ := eja_spectral x
  have hw : ∃ l ∈ s, l ≠ 0 := by
    by_contra hcon
    simp only [not_exists, not_and, ne_eq, not_not] at hcon
    refine hx ?_
    rw [hdec]
    refine Finset.sum_eq_zero ?_
    intro l hl
    rw [hcon l hl, zero_smul]
  obtain ⟨l0, hl0, hl0ne⟩ := hw
  set p : V := e l0 with hp
  have hpi : p * p = p := hidem l0 hl0
  have hpne : p ≠ 0 := hne0 l0 hl0
  have hτ : 0 < ejaTrL p := eja_tr_idem_pos p hpi hpne
  refine ⟨(ejaTrL p)⁻¹ • (ejaTrL.comp (ejaLm p)), ?_, ?_, ?_⟩
  · intro y hy
    have h1 : ((ejaTrL p)⁻¹ • (ejaTrL.comp (ejaLm p))) y = (ejaTrL p)⁻¹ * ejaB y p := by
      simp only [LinearMap.smul_apply, LinearMap.comp_apply, ejaLm_apply, smul_eq_mul, ejaB]
      rw [eja_mul_comm p y]
    rw [h1]
    refine mul_nonneg (by positivity) ?_
    exact eja_isSumSq_ejaB_idem_nonneg ((eja_nonneg_iff y).mp hy) p hpi
  · have h1 : ((ejaTrL p)⁻¹ • (ejaTrL.comp (ejaLm p))) 1 = (ejaTrL p)⁻¹ * ejaTrL p := by
      simp only [LinearMap.smul_apply, LinearMap.comp_apply, ejaLm_apply, smul_eq_mul]
      rw [eja_mul_one]
    rw [h1, inv_mul_cancel₀ (ne_of_gt hτ)]
  · have hpx : p * x = l0 • p := by
      rw [hdec, eja_mul_sum, Finset.sum_eq_single l0]
      · rw [eja_mul_smul, hpi]
      · intro k hk hkl
        rw [eja_mul_smul, hp, horth l0 hl0 k hk (Ne.symm hkl), smul_zero]
      · intro h'; exact absurd hl0 h'
    have h1 : ((ejaTrL p)⁻¹ • (ejaTrL.comp (ejaLm p))) x = (ejaTrL p)⁻¹ * (l0 * ejaTrL p) := by
      simp only [LinearMap.smul_apply, LinearMap.comp_apply, ejaLm_apply, smul_eq_mul]
      rw [hpx, map_smul, smul_eq_mul]
    rw [h1]
    have h2 : (ejaTrL p)⁻¹ * (l0 * ejaTrL p) = l0 := by
      field_simp
    rw [h2]
    exact hl0ne

end Spectral


/-! ### The unit is an order unit

The one substantial theorem of the file. -/

section OrderUnit

/-- A convex cone in a finite-dimensional real normed space that spans and
on which every linear functional bounded above by `0` vanishes is the whole
space.  This is the finite-dimensional Hahn–Banach separation argument,
isolated from the Jordan-algebraic input. -/
theorem convexCone_eq_univ_of_forall_functional {E : Type u}
    [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E]
    {W : Set E} (hconv : Convex ℝ W)
    (hcone : ∀ r : ℝ, 0 < r → ∀ x ∈ W, r • x ∈ W) (h0 : (0 : E) ∈ W)
    (hsub : ∀ x : E, ∃ a ∈ W, ∃ b ∈ W, x = a - b)
    (hfun : ∀ f : E →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ, (∀ w ∈ W, f w ≤ 0) → ∀ x, f x = 0) :
    ∀ x : E, x ∈ W := by
  intro x₀
  by_contra hx₀
  have hspan : affineSpan ℝ W = ⊤ := by
    have hsp : Submodule.span ℝ W = ⊤ := by
      refine Submodule.eq_top_iff'.mpr fun x => ?_
      obtain ⟨a, ha, b, hb, rfl⟩ := hsub x
      exact Submodule.sub_mem _ (Submodule.subset_span ha) (Submodule.subset_span hb)
    have hne : ((affineSpan ℝ W : AffineSubspace ℝ E) : Set E).Nonempty :=
      ⟨0, subset_affineSpan ℝ W h0⟩
    rw [← AffineSubspace.direction_eq_top_iff_of_nonempty hne, direction_affineSpan,
      vectorSpan_eq_span_vsub_set_right ℝ h0]
    have himg : ((fun x => x -ᵥ (0 : E)) '' W) = W := by
      ext y
      simp
    rw [himg]
    exact hsp
  obtain ⟨v₀, hv₀⟩ := hconv.interior_nonempty_iff_affineSpan_eq_top.mpr hspan
  obtain ⟨f, u, hf1, hf2⟩ := geometric_hahn_banach_open (E := E)
    hconv.interior isOpen_interior (convex_singleton x₀)
    (Set.disjoint_singleton_right.mpr fun h => hx₀ (interior_subset h))
  have hfx₀ : u ≤ f x₀ := hf2 x₀ rfl
  have hle : ∀ w ∈ W, f w ≤ 0 := by
    intro w hw
    by_contra hcon
    have hpos : 0 < f w := not_le.mp hcon
    set s : ℝ := max 1 ((2 * u - f v₀ + 1) / f w) with hsdef
    have hs1 : (0 : ℝ) < s := lt_of_lt_of_le zero_lt_one (le_max_left _ _)
    have hsw : s • w ∈ W := hcone s hs1 w hw
    have hmem : (2 : ℝ)⁻¹ • v₀ + (2 : ℝ)⁻¹ • (s • w) ∈ interior W :=
      hconv.combo_interior_closure_mem_interior hv₀ (subset_closure hsw)
        (by norm_num) (by norm_num) (by norm_num)
    have hlt := hf1 _ hmem
    rw [map_add, map_smul, map_smul, map_smul, smul_eq_mul, smul_eq_mul,
      smul_eq_mul] at hlt
    have hge : (2 * u - f v₀ + 1) / f w ≤ s := le_max_right _ _
    have hmul : 2 * u - f v₀ + 1 ≤ s * f w := (div_le_iff₀ hpos).mp hge
    linarith
  have hzero := hfun f.toLinearMap hle
  have h1 : f v₀ = 0 := hzero v₀
  have h2 : f x₀ = 0 := hzero x₀
  have h3 := hf1 v₀ hv₀
  linarith

variable {V : Type u} [AddCommGroup V] [Module ℝ V] [Mul V] [One V]
  [PartialOrder V] [EuclideanJordanAlgebra V]

/-- The elements of a Euclidean Jordan algebra that are below some natural
multiple of the unit.  The order-unit theorem says this is everything. -/
def ejaBdd (V : Type u) [AddCommGroup V] [Module ℝ V] [Mul V] [One V]
    [PartialOrder V] [EuclideanJordanAlgebra V] : Set V :=
  {y : V | ∃ n : ℕ, IsSumSq ((n : ℝ) • (1 : V) - y)}

theorem eja_neg_mem_bdd {s : V} (hs : IsSumSq s) : -s ∈ ejaBdd V := by
  refine ⟨0, ?_⟩
  simpa using hs

theorem eja_zero_mem_bdd : (0 : V) ∈ ejaBdd V := by
  refine ⟨0, ?_⟩
  simp

theorem eja_one_mem_bdd : (1 : V) ∈ ejaBdd V := by
  refine ⟨1, ?_⟩
  simp

theorem eja_bdd_add {x y : V} (hx : x ∈ ejaBdd V) (hy : y ∈ ejaBdd V) :
    x + y ∈ ejaBdd V := by
  obtain ⟨n, hn⟩ := hx
  obtain ⟨m, hm⟩ := hy
  refine ⟨n + m, ?_⟩
  have he : ((n + m : ℕ) : ℝ) • (1 : V) - (x + y)
      = ((n : ℝ) • (1 : V) - x) + ((m : ℝ) • (1 : V) - y) := by
    push_cast
    rw [add_smul]
    abel
  rw [he]
  exact hn.add hm

theorem eja_bdd_smul {r : ℝ} (hr : 0 ≤ r) {x : V} (hx : x ∈ ejaBdd V) :
    r • x ∈ ejaBdd V := by
  obtain ⟨n, hn⟩ := hx
  refine ⟨⌈r * n⌉₊, ?_⟩
  have h₁ : IsSumSq (r • ((n : ℝ) • (1 : V) - x)) := eja_isSumSq_smul hr hn
  have h₂ : IsSumSq (((⌈r * n⌉₊ : ℝ) - r * n) • (1 : V)) :=
    eja_isSumSq_smul_one (by
      have := Nat.le_ceil (r * (n : ℝ))
      linarith)
  have he : ((⌈r * n⌉₊ : ℕ) : ℝ) • (1 : V) - r • x
      = (((⌈r * n⌉₊ : ℝ) - r * n) • (1 : V)) + r • ((n : ℝ) • (1 : V) - x) := by
    rw [smul_sub, sub_smul, smul_smul]
    abel
  rw [he]
  exact h₂.add h₁

theorem eja_bdd_convex : Convex ℝ (ejaBdd V) := fun _ hx _ hy _ _ ha hb _ =>
  eja_bdd_add (eja_bdd_smul ha hx) (eja_bdd_smul hb hy)

theorem eja_bdd_sub (x : V) :
    ∃ a ∈ ejaBdd V, ∃ b ∈ ejaBdd V, x = a - b := by
  refine ⟨-((4 : ℝ)⁻¹ • ((x - 1) * (x - 1))),
    eja_neg_mem_bdd (eja_isSumSq_smul (by norm_num) (IsSumSq.mul_self _)),
    -((4 : ℝ)⁻¹ • ((x + 1) * (x + 1))),
    eja_neg_mem_bdd (eja_isSumSq_smul (by norm_num) (IsSumSq.mul_self _)), ?_⟩
  have key : (4 : ℝ)⁻¹ • ((x + 1) * (x + 1)) - (4 : ℝ)⁻¹ • ((x - 1) * (x - 1)) = x := by
    rw [← smul_sub, ← eja_four_smul x, smul_smul]
    norm_num
  rw [neg_sub_neg]
  exact key.symm

/-- Every linear functional that is `≤ 0` on `ejaBdd V` vanishes: it is
`≥ 0` on squares and kills `1`, so `0 ≤ f(a * a) + 2t · f(a)` for all real
`t`. -/
theorem eja_functional_eq_zero (f : V →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ)
    (hf : ∀ w ∈ ejaBdd V, f w ≤ 0) : ∀ x, f x = 0 := by
  have hsq : ∀ a : V, 0 ≤ f (a * a) := by
    intro a
    have h := hf _ (eja_neg_mem_bdd (IsSumSq.mul_self a))
    rw [map_neg] at h
    linarith
  have hone : f 1 = 0 := by
    have h₁ := hf _ (eja_one_mem_bdd (V := V))
    have h₂ := hf _ (eja_neg_mem_bdd (eja_isSumSq_one (V := V)))
    rw [map_neg] at h₂
    linarith
  intro a
  have hquad : ∀ t : ℝ, 0 ≤ f (a * a) + t * f a + t * f a := by
    intro t
    have he : (a + t • (1 : V)) * (a + t • (1 : V))
        = a * a + t • a + (t • a + (t * t) • (1 : V)) := by
      rw [eja_add_mul_add, eja_mul_smul, eja_mul_one, eja_smul_mul, eja_one_mul,
        eja_smul_mul, eja_mul_smul, eja_mul_one, smul_smul]
    have h := hsq (a + t • (1 : V))
    rw [he] at h
    simp only [map_add, map_smul, smul_eq_mul, hone, mul_zero, add_zero] at h
    linarith
  by_contra hfa
  have hd : (-(f (a * a) + 1) / (2 * f a)) * f a = -(f (a * a) + 1) / 2 := by
    field_simp
  have key := hquad (-(f (a * a) + 1) / (2 * f a))
  rw [hd] at key
  linarith

/-- **The unit of a Euclidean Jordan algebra is an order unit**: every
element is below some natural multiple of `1`.  Proved by separation (see
the file header); this is where finite-dimensionality is used. -/
theorem eja_exists_isSumSq_nsmul_one_sub (x : V) :
    ∃ n : ℕ, IsSumSq ((n : ℝ) • (1 : V) - x) := by
  classical
  obtain ⟨e⟩ : Nonempty (V ≃ₗ[ℝ] (Module.Free.ChooseBasisIndex ℝ V → ℝ)) :=
    ⟨(Module.Free.chooseBasis ℝ V).equivFun⟩
  have hSconv : Convex ℝ (⇑e.symm ⁻¹' ejaBdd V) := by
    simpa using (eja_bdd_convex (V := V)).linear_preimage
      (e.symm : (Module.Free.ChooseBasisIndex ℝ V → ℝ) →ₗ[ℝ] V)
  have hScone : ∀ r : ℝ, 0 < r → ∀ y ∈ ⇑e.symm ⁻¹' ejaBdd V,
      r • y ∈ ⇑e.symm ⁻¹' ejaBdd V := by
    intro r hr y hy
    have hsm : e.symm (r • y) = r • e.symm y := map_smul _ _ _
    show e.symm (r • y) ∈ ejaBdd V
    rw [hsm]
    exact eja_bdd_smul hr.le hy
  have hS0 : (0 : Module.Free.ChooseBasisIndex ℝ V → ℝ) ∈ ⇑e.symm ⁻¹' ejaBdd V := by
    show e.symm 0 ∈ ejaBdd V
    rw [map_zero]
    exact eja_zero_mem_bdd
  have hSsub : ∀ y : (Module.Free.ChooseBasisIndex ℝ V → ℝ),
      ∃ a ∈ ⇑e.symm ⁻¹' ejaBdd V, ∃ b ∈ ⇑e.symm ⁻¹' ejaBdd V, y = a - b := by
    intro y
    obtain ⟨a, ha, c, hc, hy⟩ := eja_bdd_sub (e.symm y)
    refine ⟨e a, ?_, e c, ?_, ?_⟩
    · show e.symm (e a) ∈ ejaBdd V
      rwa [e.symm_apply_apply]
    · show e.symm (e c) ∈ ejaBdd V
      rwa [e.symm_apply_apply]
    · have hc2 := congrArg e hy
      rw [e.apply_symm_apply, map_sub] at hc2
      exact hc2
  have hSfun : ∀ f : (Module.Free.ChooseBasisIndex ℝ V → ℝ) →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ,
      (∀ w ∈ ⇑e.symm ⁻¹' ejaBdd V, f w ≤ 0) → ∀ y, f y = 0 := by
    intro f hf y
    have hg : ∀ w ∈ ejaBdd V, (f.comp (e : V →ₗ[ℝ] _)) w ≤ 0 := by
      intro w hw
      refine hf (e w) ?_
      show e.symm (e w) ∈ ejaBdd V
      rwa [e.symm_apply_apply]
    have hgz := eja_functional_eq_zero (f.comp (e : V →ₗ[ℝ] _)) hg (e.symm y)
    have hgz2 : f (e (e.symm y)) = 0 := hgz
    rwa [e.apply_symm_apply] at hgz2
  have hall := convexCone_eq_univ_of_forall_functional hSconv hScone hS0 hSsub hSfun
  have hx : e.symm (e x) ∈ ejaBdd V := hall (e x)
  rw [e.symm_apply_apply] at hx
  exact hx

/-- **A Euclidean Jordan algebra is an order unit space** (in the sense of
`OrderUnit.lean`), with the cone of sums of squares and the Jordan unit as
order unit.  Not an instance: `ℝ`, `ULift ℝ` and products already carry
their own `OrderUnitSpace` structures, and this one coincides with them
(the order is fixed by `le_iff` and the unit by `unit = 1`). -/
def toOrderUnitSpace (V : Type u) [AddCommGroup V] [Module ℝ V] [Mul V]
    [One V] [PartialOrder V] [EuclideanJordanAlgebra V] : OrderUnitSpace V where
  add_le_add_left x y h z := by
    rw [eja_le_iff] at h ⊢
    have he : y + z - (x + z) = y - x := by abel
    rw [he]
    exact h
  smul_nonneg hr hx := by
    rw [eja_nonneg_iff] at hx ⊢
    exact eja_isSumSq_smul hr hx
  unit := 1
  exists_le_smul_unit x := by
    obtain ⟨n, hn⟩ := eja_exists_isSumSq_nsmul_one_sub x
    exact ⟨n, (eja_le_iff _ _).mpr hn⟩

end OrderUnit

/-! ### The basic examples of Euclidean Jordan algebras -/

/-- `ℝ` is a Euclidean Jordan algebra: the one-dimensional one. -/
instance _root_.Real.euclideanJordanAlgebra : EuclideanJordanAlgebra ℝ where
  mul_comm := mul_comm
  mul_add := mul_add
  smul_mul r a b := by simp [smul_eq_mul, mul_assoc]
  one_mul := one_mul
  lmul_comm_rmul_rmul a b := by ring
  finiteDimensional := inferInstance
  formallyReal {a s} hs h := by
    have h₁ : 0 ≤ s := IsSumSq.nonneg hs
    have h₂ : 0 ≤ a * a := mul_self_nonneg a
    have : a * a = 0 := by linarith
    exact mul_self_eq_zero.mp this
  le_iff x y := by
    constructor
    · intro h
      have h₀ : 0 ≤ y - x := sub_nonneg.mpr h
      have := IsSumSq.mul_self (Real.sqrt (y - x))
      rwa [Real.mul_self_sqrt h₀] at this
    · intro h
      have := IsSumSq.nonneg h
      linarith

/-- A `ULift` of a Euclidean Jordan algebra is one (needed because the
objects of `OUS` live in a single universe). -/
instance ULift.euclideanJordanAlgebra (V : Type u) [AddCommGroup V]
    [Module ℝ V] [Mul V] [One V] [PartialOrder V] [EuclideanJordanAlgebra V] :
    EuclideanJordanAlgebra (ULift.{v} V) where
  mul_comm a b := ULift.down_injective (eja_mul_comm a.down b.down)
  mul_add a b c := ULift.down_injective (eja_mul_add a.down b.down c.down)
  smul_mul r a b := ULift.down_injective (eja_smul_mul r a.down b.down)
  one_mul a := ULift.down_injective (eja_one_mul a.down)
  lmul_comm_rmul_rmul a b :=
    ULift.down_injective (EuclideanJordanAlgebra.lmul_comm_rmul_rmul a.down b.down)
  finiteDimensional := Module.Finite.equiv (ULift.moduleEquiv (R := ℝ) (M := V)).symm
  formallyReal {a s} hs h := by
    refine ULift.down_injective ?_
    refine EuclideanJordanAlgebra.formallyReal (isSumSq_ulift_iff.mp hs) ?_
    exact congrArg ULift.down h
  le_iff x y := by
    rw [isSumSq_ulift_iff]
    exact eja_le_iff x.down y.down

/-- The one-point algebra (in which `1 = 0`) is a Euclidean Jordan
algebra; it is the final object of `EJA`, hence the initial object of
`EJAᵒᵖ`. -/
instance PUnit.euclideanJordanAlgebra :
    EuclideanJordanAlgebra PUnit.{u + 1} where
  mul_comm _ _ := Subsingleton.elim _ _
  mul_add _ _ _ := Subsingleton.elim _ _
  smul_mul _ _ _ := Subsingleton.elim _ _
  one_mul _ := Subsingleton.elim _ _
  lmul_comm_rmul_rmul _ _ := Subsingleton.elim _ _
  finiteDimensional := by
    refine Module.Finite.of_surjective (0 : ℝ →ₗ[ℝ] PUnit.{u + 1}) ?_
    intro y
    exact ⟨0, Subsingleton.elim _ _⟩
  formallyReal _ _ := Subsingleton.elim _ _
  le_iff x y := by
    constructor
    · intro _
      have he : y - x = 0 := Subsingleton.elim _ _
      rw [he]
      exact IsSumSq.zero
    · intro _
      exact le_of_eq (Subsingleton.elim _ _)

/-- A product of two Euclidean Jordan algebras is one; this is the binary
coproduct of `EJAᵒᵖ`. -/
instance Prod.euclideanJordanAlgebra (V : Type u) (W : Type u) [AddCommGroup V]
    [Module ℝ V] [Mul V] [One V] [PartialOrder V] [EuclideanJordanAlgebra V]
    [AddCommGroup W] [Module ℝ W] [Mul W] [One W] [PartialOrder W]
    [EuclideanJordanAlgebra W] : EuclideanJordanAlgebra (V × W) where
  mul_comm a b := Prod.ext (eja_mul_comm a.1 b.1) (eja_mul_comm a.2 b.2)
  mul_add a b c := Prod.ext (eja_mul_add a.1 b.1 c.1) (eja_mul_add a.2 b.2 c.2)
  smul_mul r a b := Prod.ext (eja_smul_mul r a.1 b.1) (eja_smul_mul r a.2 b.2)
  one_mul a := Prod.ext (eja_one_mul a.1) (eja_one_mul a.2)
  lmul_comm_rmul_rmul a b :=
    Prod.ext (EuclideanJordanAlgebra.lmul_comm_rmul_rmul a.1 b.1)
      (EuclideanJordanAlgebra.lmul_comm_rmul_rmul a.2 b.2)
  finiteDimensional := inferInstance
  formallyReal {a s} hs h := by
    refine Prod.ext ?_ ?_
    · exact EuclideanJordanAlgebra.formallyReal (isSumSq_fst hs) (congrArg Prod.fst h)
    · exact EuclideanJordanAlgebra.formallyReal (isSumSq_snd hs) (congrArg Prod.snd h)
  le_iff x y := by
    constructor
    · intro h
      have h₁ : IsSumSq (y.1 - x.1) := (eja_le_iff _ _).mp (Prod.le_def.mp h).1
      have h₂ : IsSumSq (y.2 - x.2) := (eja_le_iff _ _).mp (Prod.le_def.mp h).2
      have he : y - x = ((y.1 - x.1, (0 : W)) : V × W) + ((0 : V), y.2 - x.2) :=
        Prod.ext (by simp) (by simp)
      rw [he]
      exact (isSumSq_inl (eja_mul_zero (0 : W)) h₁).add (isSumSq_inr (eja_mul_zero (0 : V)) h₂)
    · intro h
      refine Prod.le_def.mpr ⟨(eja_le_iff _ _).mpr ?_, (eja_le_iff _ _).mpr ?_⟩
      · exact isSumSq_fst h
      · exact isSumSq_snd h

/-! ### The textbook ("Euclidean") form of the definition

A Euclidean Jordan algebra is usually presented as a finite-dimensional
real unital commutative Jordan algebra carrying an inner product with
`⟪x * y, z⟫ = ⟪y, x * z⟫` — the trace form.  The two lemmas below turn
that presentation into the class above: such a form makes the algebra
formally real (`formallyReal_of_form`), and the cone of sums of squares
then carries the order (`sumSqPartialOrder`), so the textbook data
produces an object of the category (`EJAObj.ofForm`). -/

/-- Multiplication by `0` is `0` in any algebra with an additive
multiplication. -/
theorem mul_zero_of_mul_add {V : Type u} [AddCommGroup V] [Mul V]
    (hadd : ∀ a b c : V, a * (b + c) = a * b + a * c) (a : V) :
    a * (0 : V) = 0 := by
  have h := hadd a 0 0
  rw [add_zero] at h
  have h2 : a * (0 : V) + 0 = a * (0 : V) + a * (0 : V) := by
    rw [add_zero]
    exact h
  exact (add_left_cancel h2).symm

/-- **A positive definite associative bilinear form makes a Jordan algebra
formally real.**  Indeed `B (a * a) 1 = B a (a * 1) = B a a ≥ 0`, so the
value of `B (·) 1` on a sum of squares is a sum of the numbers `B aᵢ aᵢ`;
if that sum vanishes each of them does, and definiteness gives
`aᵢ = 0`. -/
theorem formallyReal_of_form {V : Type u} [AddCommGroup V] [Module ℝ V]
    [Mul V] [One V] (B : V →ₗ[ℝ] V →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ)
    (hassoc : ∀ x y z : V, B (x * y) z = B y (x * z))
    (hone : ∀ a : V, a * 1 = a) (hpos : ∀ x : V, 0 ≤ B x x)
    (hdef : ∀ x : V, B x x = 0 → x = 0) :
    ∀ {a s : V}, IsSumSq s → a * a + s = 0 → a = 0 := by
  have hsq : ∀ x : V, B (x * x) 1 = B x x := by
    intro x
    rw [hassoc, hone]
  have hsum : ∀ s : V, IsSumSq s → 0 ≤ B s 1 := by
    intro s hs
    induction hs with
    | zero => simp
    | @sq_add a t _ ih =>
      have he : B (a * a + t) 1 = B (a * a) 1 + B t 1 := by
        rw [map_add]
        rfl
      rw [he, hsq]
      have := hpos a
      linarith
  intro a s hs h
  have h0 : B (a * a + s) 1 = 0 := by
    rw [h]
    simp
  have he : B (a * a + s) 1 = B a a + B s 1 := by
    rw [map_add]
    have : (B (a * a) + B s) 1 = B (a * a) 1 + B s 1 := rfl
    rw [this, hsq]
  have h1 := hpos a
  have h2 := hsum s hs
  refine hdef a ?_
  rw [he] at h0
  linarith

/-- **The textbook data makes a Euclidean Jordan algebra** in the sense of
the class above, with the order of the cone of sums of squares. -/
theorem ofForm (V : Type u) [AddCommGroup V] [Module ℝ V] [Mul V] [One V]
    (hcomm : ∀ a b : V, a * b = b * a)
    (hadd : ∀ a b c : V, a * (b + c) = a * b + a * c)
    (hsmul : ∀ (r : ℝ) (a b : V), (r • a) * b = r • (a * b))
    (hone : ∀ a : V, 1 * a = a)
    (hjordan : ∀ a b : V, a * b * (a * a) = a * (b * (a * a)))
    (hfin : FiniteDimensional ℝ V) (B : V →ₗ[ℝ] V →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ)
    (hassoc : ∀ x y z : V, B (x * y) z = B y (x * z))
    (hpos : ∀ x : V, 0 ≤ B x x) (hdef : ∀ x : V, B x x = 0 → x = 0) :
    @EuclideanJordanAlgebra V _ _ _ _
      (sumSqPartialOrder V
        (mul_zero_of_mul_add hadd 0)
        (formallyReal_of_form B hassoc (fun a => (hcomm a 1).trans (hone a)) hpos
          hdef)) :=
  letI := sumSqPartialOrder V
    (mul_zero_of_mul_add hadd 0)
    (formallyReal_of_form B hassoc (fun a => (hcomm a 1).trans (hone a)) hpos hdef)
  { mul_comm := hcomm
    mul_add := hadd
    smul_mul := hsmul
    one_mul := hone
    lmul_comm_rmul_rmul := hjordan
    finiteDimensional := hfin
    formallyReal := formallyReal_of_form B hassoc
      (fun a => (hcomm a 1).trans (hone a)) hpos hdef
    le_iff := fun _ _ => Iff.rfl }

end EuclideanJordanAlgebra

/-! ## The category `EJA` (189aIII)

`EJA` is the full subcategory of `OUS` spanned by the order unit spaces
whose order is the cone of squares of a Euclidean Jordan structure and
whose order unit is its Jordan unit; the morphisms are, as in `OUS`, all
positive unit-preserving linear maps, which for these objects are exactly
the positive unital maps of Euclidean Jordan algebras. -/

open EuclideanJordanAlgebra

/-- Carrying a Euclidean Jordan structure whose cone of sums of squares is
the order and whose unit is the order unit, as a property of the objects
of `OUS`. -/
def IsEJA : ObjectProperty OUS.{u} := fun X =>
  ∃ (_ : Mul X.carrier) (_ : One X.carrier),
    ∃ _ : EuclideanJordanAlgebra X.carrier, (1 : X.carrier) = X.unit

/-- The way `IsEJA` is verified: exhibit the instances. -/
theorem isEJA_of (X : OUS.{u}) [Mul X.carrier] [One X.carrier]
    [EuclideanJordanAlgebra X.carrier] (h : (1 : X.carrier) = X.unit) :
    IsEJA X := ⟨inferInstance, inferInstance, inferInstance, h⟩

/-- **`EJA`**: the full subcategory of `OUS` spanned by the Euclidean
Jordan algebras. -/
abbrev EJAObj : Type (u + 1) := IsEJA.{u}.FullSubcategory

/-- **Every Euclidean Jordan algebra is an object of `EJA`**: its cone of
sums of squares and its Jordan unit make it an order unit space
(`EuclideanJordanAlgebra.toOrderUnitSpace`, whose order-unit axiom is the
separation theorem `eja_exists_isSumSq_nsmul_one_sub`), and that order and
unit are by construction the ones `IsEJA` asks for.  Together with fullness
this is what makes `EJA` the category of Euclidean Jordan algebras with
positive unital maps: the objects are exactly the order unit spaces of
Euclidean Jordan algebras and the morphisms are exactly the positive
unital linear maps between them, so `EJA` is equivalent to the category
whose objects are Euclidean Jordan structures themselves (two Jordan
structures with the same order and unit are, in that category, isomorphic
by the identity). -/
def EJAObj.of (V : Type u) [AddCommGroup V] [Module ℝ V] [Mul V] [One V]
    [PartialOrder V] [EuclideanJordanAlgebra V] : EJAObj.{u} :=
  letI := EuclideanJordanAlgebra.toOrderUnitSpace V
  ⟨OUS.of V, isEJA_of (OUS.of V) rfl⟩

/-- **Every Euclidean Jordan algebra in the usual, trace-form sense is an
object of `EJA`**: a finite-dimensional real unital commutative Jordan
algebra with a positive definite bilinear form satisfying
`B (x * y) z = B y (x * z)`, ordered by its cone of sums of squares.  This
and `EJAObj.of` are what pin `EJA` down as the category eff.tex names. -/
def EJAObj.ofForm (V : Type u) [AddCommGroup V] [Module ℝ V] [Mul V] [One V]
    (hcomm : ∀ a b : V, a * b = b * a)
    (hadd : ∀ a b c : V, a * (b + c) = a * b + a * c)
    (hsmul : ∀ (r : ℝ) (a b : V), (r • a) * b = r • (a * b))
    (hone : ∀ a : V, 1 * a = a)
    (hjordan : ∀ a b : V, a * b * (a * a) = a * (b * (a * a)))
    (hfin : FiniteDimensional ℝ V) (B : V →ₗ[ℝ] V →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ)
    (hassoc : ∀ x y z : V, B (x * y) z = B y (x * z))
    (hpos : ∀ x : V, 0 ≤ B x x) (hdef : ∀ x : V, B x x = 0 → x = 0) :
    EJAObj.{u} :=
  letI := sumSqPartialOrder V
    (EuclideanJordanAlgebra.mul_zero_of_mul_add hadd 0)
    (EuclideanJordanAlgebra.formallyReal_of_form B hassoc
      (fun a => (hcomm a 1).trans (hone a)) hpos hdef)
  letI := EuclideanJordanAlgebra.ofForm V hcomm hadd hsmul hone hjordan hfin B
    hassoc hpos hdef
  EJAObj.of V

/-- The scalars `ℝᵤ` as an object of `EJA`. -/
def ejaScal : EJAObj.{u} :=
  ⟨ousScal.{u}, isEJA_of ousScal.{u} rfl⟩

/-- The product of two Euclidean Jordan algebras, as an object of `EJA`
(the effectus coproduct of `EJAᵒᵖ`). -/
def ejaProd (X Y : EJAObj.{u}) : EJAObj.{u} := by
  refine ⟨X.obj.prod Y.obj, ?_⟩
  obtain ⟨mX, oX, eX, hX⟩ := X.property
  obtain ⟨mY, oY, eY, hY⟩ := Y.property
  let _ := mX; let _ := oX; let _ := eX; let _ := mY; let _ := oY; let _ := eY
  exact isEJA_of (X.obj.prod Y.obj) (Prod.ext hX hY)

/-- The one-point algebra as an object of `EJA`. -/
def ejaTriv : EJAObj.{u} :=
  ⟨ousTriv.{u}, isEJA_of ousTriv.{u} (Subsingleton.elim _ _)⟩

theorem eja_hom_ext {X Y : EJAObj.{u}} {f g : X ⟶ Y}
    (h : ∀ x, f.hom.toLinearMap x = g.hom.toLinearMap x) : f = g :=
  InducedCategory.hom_ext (ous_hom_ext h)

theorem ejaop_hom_ext {X Y : EJAObj.{u}ᵒᵖ} {f g : X ⟶ Y}
    (h : ∀ x, f.unop.hom.toLinearMap x = g.unop.hom.toLinearMap x) : f = g :=
  Quiver.Hom.unop_inj (eja_hom_ext h)

theorem ejaop_comp_apply {X Y Z : EJAObj.{u}ᵒᵖ} (f : X ⟶ Y) (g : Y ⟶ Z)
    (x : Z.unop.obj.carrier) :
    (f ≫ g).unop.hom.toLinearMap x
      = f.unop.hom.toLinearMap (g.unop.hom.toLinearMap x) :=
  ous_comp_apply g.unop.hom f.unop.hom x

theorem ejaop_congr {X Y : EJAObj.{u}ᵒᵖ} {f g : X ⟶ Y} (h : f = g)
    (x : Y.unop.obj.carrier) :
    f.unop.hom.toLinearMap x = g.unop.hom.toLinearMap x := by rw [h]

theorem eja_id_apply {X : EJAObj.{u}} (x : X.obj.carrier) :
    (𝟙 X : X ⟶ X).hom.toLinearMap x = x := rfl

/-- Postcomposition with a fixed map, pointwise. -/
theorem ejaop_comp_congr {Z P X : EJAObj.{u}ᵒᵖ} {F : P ⟶ X} {a b : Z ⟶ P}
    (h : a ≫ F = b ≫ F) (x : X.unop.obj.carrier) :
    a.unop.hom.toLinearMap (F.unop.hom.toLinearMap x)
      = b.unop.hom.toLinearMap (F.unop.hom.toLinearMap x) :=
  ((ejaop_comp_apply a F x).symm.trans (ejaop_congr h x)).trans
    (ejaop_comp_apply b F x)

/-- `ℝᵤ` is initial in `EJA`, hence final in `EJAᵒᵖ`. -/
def ejaScalIsInitial : IsInitial (ejaScal.{u}) :=
  IsInitial.ofUniqueHom (fun X => InducedCategory.homMk (ousUnitMap X.obj))
    (fun _ f => InducedCategory.hom_ext (ousUnitMap_unique f.hom))

/-- The one-point algebra is final in `EJA`, hence initial in `EJAᵒᵖ`. -/
def ejaTrivIsTerminal : IsTerminal (ejaTriv.{u}) :=
  IsTerminal.ofUniqueHom (fun X => InducedCategory.homMk (ousTrivMap X.obj))
    (fun _ _ => InducedCategory.hom_ext
      (ous_hom_ext fun _ => Subsingleton.elim (α := PUnit.{u + 1}) _ _))

/-- The concrete presentation of `EJAᵒᵖ`, the restriction of `ousPres`:
the final object is `ℝᵤ`, the binary coproducts are the products. -/
def ejaPres : CoprodPres (EJAObj.{u}ᵒᵖ) where
  T := Opposite.op ejaScal
  hT := IsInitial.op (EJAObj.{u}) ejaScalIsInitial
  P X Y := Opposite.op (ejaProd X.unop Y.unop)
  pinl X Y := Quiver.Hom.op
    (InducedCategory.homMk (ousFst X.unop.obj Y.unop.obj))
  pinr X Y := Quiver.Hom.op
    (InducedCategory.homMk (ousSnd X.unop.obj Y.unop.obj))
  hP X Y := BinaryCofan.IsColimit.mk _
    (fun {_} u v => Quiver.Hom.op
      (InducedCategory.homMk (ousPair u.unop.hom v.unop.hom)))
    (fun {_} _ _ => ejaop_hom_ext fun x => ejaop_comp_apply _ _ x)
    (fun {_} _ _ => ejaop_hom_ext fun x => ejaop_comp_apply _ _ x)
    (fun {_} _ _ m h₁ h₂ => ejaop_hom_ext fun x => by
      refine Prod.ext ?_ ?_
      · exact (ejaop_comp_apply _ m x).symm.trans (ejaop_congr h₁ x)
      · exact (ejaop_comp_apply _ m x).symm.trans (ejaop_congr h₂ x))

/-- The unique morphism into the final object of `EJAᵒᵖ` is `r ↦ r · 1`. -/
theorem ejaPres_from_apply (Y : EJAObj.{u}ᵒᵖ) (r : ULift.{u} ℝ) :
    (ejaPres.hT.from Y).unop.hom.toLinearMap r = r.down • Y.unop.obj.unit := by
  have h : ejaPres.hT.from Y
      = Quiver.Hom.op (InducedCategory.homMk (ousUnitMap Y.unop.obj)) :=
    ejaPres.hT.hom_ext _ _
  rw [h]
  rfl

/-- The coproduct of two morphisms of `EJAᵒᵖ` acts coordinatewise. -/
theorem ejaPres_pmap_apply {X X' Y Y' : EJAObj.{u}ᵒᵖ} (f : X ⟶ X') (g : Y ⟶ Y')
    (p : (ejaPres.P X' Y').unop.obj.carrier) :
    (ejaPres.pmap f g).unop.hom.toLinearMap p
      = (f.unop.hom.toLinearMap p.1, g.unop.hom.toLinearMap p.2) := by
  refine Prod.ext ?_ ?_
  · exact ejaop_comp_apply f _ p
  · exact ejaop_comp_apply g _ p


/-- `EJAᵒᵖ` has a final object, `ℝᵤ`. -/
instance ejaOpHasTerminal : HasTerminal (EJAObj.{u}ᵒᵖ) := ejaPres.hT.hasTerminal

/-- `EJAᵒᵖ` has an initial object, the one-point algebra. -/
instance ejaOpHasInitial : HasInitial (EJAObj.{u}ᵒᵖ) :=
  (IsTerminal.op (EJAObj.{u}) ejaTrivIsTerminal).hasInitial

/-- The binary coproducts of `EJAᵒᵖ` are the products. -/
instance ejaOpHasColimitPair (X Y : EJAObj.{u}ᵒᵖ) : HasColimit (pair X Y) :=
  HasColimit.mk ⟨_, ejaPres.hP X Y⟩

/-- `EJAᵒᵖ` has binary coproducts. -/
instance ejaOpHasBinaryCoproducts : HasBinaryCoproducts (EJAObj.{u}ᵒᵖ) :=
  hasBinaryCoproducts_of_hasColimit_pair _

/-- `EJAᵒᵖ` has finite coproducts. -/
instance ejaOpHasFiniteCoproducts : HasFiniteCoproducts (EJAObj.{u}ᵒᵖ) :=
  hasFiniteCoproducts_of_has_binary_and_initial

/-- **189aIII** (`effexamplesintro`, eff.tex:2063, Examples): the three
axioms of 180I for `EJAᵒᵖ`, as an instance; `effectus_eja` bundles it.  It
is needed as an instance by the `Pred` and `Stat` of 190V below. -/
instance effectusTotalForm_eja : EffectusTotalForm EJAObj.{u}ᵒᵖ := by
  refine effectusTotalForm_of_pres ejaPres ?_ ?_ ?_
  · intro X Y
    refine IsPullback.of_map_of_faithful (IsEJA.{u}.ι.op) ?_
    have e₁ : (IsEJA.{u}.ι.op).map (ejaPres.pmap (𝟙 X) (ejaPres.hT.from Y))
        = Quiver.Hom.op (ousSq1i X.unop.obj Y.unop.obj) := by
      refine ousop_hom_ext fun p => (ejaPres_pmap_apply _ _ p).trans ?_
      refine Prod.ext rfl ?_
      exact ejaPres_from_apply Y p.2
    have e₂ : (IsEJA.{u}.ι.op).map (ejaPres.pmap (ejaPres.hT.from X) (𝟙 Y))
        = Quiver.Hom.op (ousSq1h X.unop.obj Y.unop.obj) := by
      refine ousop_hom_ext fun p => (ejaPres_pmap_apply _ _ p).trans ?_
      refine Prod.ext ?_ rfl
      exact ejaPres_from_apply X p.1
    have e₃ : (IsEJA.{u}.ι.op).map
          (ejaPres.pmap (ejaPres.hT.from X) (𝟙 ejaPres.T))
        = Quiver.Hom.op (ousSq1g X.unop.obj) := by
      refine ousop_hom_ext fun p => (ejaPres_pmap_apply _ _ p).trans ?_
      refine Prod.ext ?_ rfl
      exact ejaPres_from_apply X p.1
    have e₄ : (IsEJA.{u}.ι.op).map
          (ejaPres.pmap (𝟙 ejaPres.T) (ejaPres.hT.from Y))
        = Quiver.Hom.op (ousSq1f Y.unop.obj) := by
      refine ousop_hom_ext fun p => (ejaPres_pmap_apply _ _ p).trans ?_
      refine Prod.ext rfl ?_
      exact ejaPres_from_apply Y p.2
    rw [e₁, e₂, e₃, e₄]
    exact (ous_isPushout1 X.unop.obj Y.unop.obj).op
  · intro X Y
    refine IsPullback.of_map_of_faithful (IsEJA.{u}.ι.op) ?_
    have f₁ : (IsEJA.{u}.ι.op).map (ejaPres.hT.from X)
        = Quiver.Hom.op (ousSq2i X.unop.obj) :=
      ousop_hom_ext fun z => ejaPres_from_apply X z
    have f₄ : (IsEJA.{u}.ι.op).map
          (ejaPres.pmap (ejaPres.hT.from X) (ejaPres.hT.from Y))
        = Quiver.Hom.op (ousSq2f X.unop.obj Y.unop.obj) := by
      refine ousop_hom_ext fun p => (ejaPres_pmap_apply _ _ p).trans ?_
      refine Prod.ext ?_ ?_
      · exact ejaPres_from_apply X p.1
      · exact ejaPres_from_apply Y p.2
    rw [f₄, f₁]
    exact (ous_isPushout2 X.unop.obj Y.unop.obj).op
  · intro Z a b hf hg
    apply Quiver.Hom.unop_inj
    refine InducedCategory.hom_ext ?_
    refine ous_jointlyMonic_aux a.unop.hom b.unop.hom (fun x => ?_) (fun x => ?_)
    · exact ejaop_comp_congr hf x
    · exact ejaop_comp_congr hg x

/-- **189aIII** (`effexamplesintro`, eff.tex:2063, Examples): the category
`EJAᵒᵖ` of Euclidean Jordan algebras with positive unit-preserving linear
maps in the opposite direction is an effectus in total form.

The point gives no proof (it refers to the joint paper with van de
Wetering).  Ours restricts the presentation `ousPres` of `OUSᵒᵖ` to
`EJAᵒᵖ` — `ℝᵤ`, the one-point algebra and a product of Euclidean Jordan
algebras are Euclidean Jordan algebras, so the final object, the initial
object and the binary coproducts of `OUSᵒᵖ` stay inside the subcategory —
and then pulls the three axioms of 180I back along the inclusion
`EJAᵒᵖ ⥤ OUSᵒᵖ`, which is fully faithful and therefore reflects pullbacks
(`IsPullback.of_map_of_faithful`).  So `ous_isPushout1`, `ous_isPushout2`
and `ous_jointlyMonic_aux` are reused verbatim; the Jordan-algebraic input
is exactly that every Euclidean Jordan algebra is an order unit space
(`EuclideanJordanAlgebra.toOrderUnitSpace`). -/
theorem effectus_eja : Nonempty (EffectusTotalStructure EJAObj.{u}ᵒᵖ) :=
  ⟨{ hasFiniteCoproducts := inferInstance
     hasTerminal := inferInstance
     effectus := inferInstance }⟩


/-! ## Predicates, states and separation for `EJAᵒᵖ` (190V)

`EJA` is a *full* subcategory of `OUS` containing `ℝᵤ`, the one-point algebra
and closed under binary products, so the concrete presentation of `OUSᵒᵖ`
restricts and the identification of predicates with effects and of states with
positive unital functionals is the one of `OrderUnit.lean`, transported along
the hom-bijection `InducedCategory.homMk`. -/

section EJAExample

open Opposite

local instance instHasFiniteCoproductsParEJA :
    HasFiniteCoproducts (Par EJAObj.{u}ᵒᵖ) := parHasFiniteCoproducts

/-- The hom-sets of `EJA` are those of `OUS`: `EJA` is a full subcategory. -/
def ejaHomEquiv {X Y : EJAObj.{u}} : (X ⟶ Y) ≃ (X.obj ⟶ Y.obj) where
  toFun f := f.hom
  invFun g := InducedCategory.homMk g
  left_inv _ := rfl
  right_inv _ := rfl

/-- The unique map `ℝᵤ ⟶ X` of `EJA`. -/
def ejaUnitMapE (X : EJAObj.{u}) : ejaScal.{u} ⟶ X :=
  InducedCategory.homMk (ousUnitMap X.obj)

theorem ejaUnitMapE_unique {X : EJAObj.{u}} (f : ejaScal.{u} ⟶ X) : f = ejaUnitMapE X :=
  InducedCategory.hom_ext (ousUnitMap_unique f.hom)

/-- The first projection of a product of Euclidean Jordan algebras. -/
def ejaFst (X Y : EJAObj.{u}) : ejaProd X Y ⟶ X :=
  InducedCategory.homMk (ousFst X.obj Y.obj)

/-- The second projection of a product of Euclidean Jordan algebras. -/
def ejaSnd (X Y : EJAObj.{u}) : ejaProd X Y ⟶ Y :=
  InducedCategory.homMk (ousSnd X.obj Y.obj)

/-- The pairing of two maps of `EJA`. -/
def ejaPair {Z X Y : EJAObj.{u}} (f : Z ⟶ X) (g : Z ⟶ Y) : Z ⟶ ejaProd X Y :=
  InducedCategory.homMk (ousPair f.hom g.hom)

theorem ejaPair_fst {Z X Y : EJAObj.{u}} (f : Z ⟶ X) (g : Z ⟶ Y) :
    ejaPair f g ≫ ejaFst X Y = f :=
  InducedCategory.hom_ext (ousPair_fst f.hom g.hom)

theorem ejaPair_snd {Z X Y : EJAObj.{u}} (f : Z ⟶ X) (g : Z ⟶ Y) :
    ejaPair f g ≫ ejaSnd X Y = g :=
  InducedCategory.hom_ext (ousPair_snd f.hom g.hom)

theorem ejaPair_eta {Z X Y : EJAObj.{u}} (m : Z ⟶ ejaProd X Y) :
    ejaPair (m ≫ ejaFst X Y) (m ≫ ejaSnd X Y) = m :=
  InducedCategory.hom_ext (ousPair_eta m.hom)

/-- The final object of `EJAᵒᵖ`, read as a Euclidean Jordan algebra. -/
abbrev ejaTopO : EJAObj.{u} := (⊤_ EJAObj.{u}ᵒᵖ).unop

/-- The unique map out of `ejaTopO`. -/
def ejaTopTo (X : EJAObj.{u}) : ejaTopO.{u} ⟶ X := (terminal.from (op X)).unop

/-- `ejaTopO` is initial in `EJA`. -/
theorem ejaTopO_hom_unique {X : EJAObj.{u}} (f g : ejaTopO.{u} ⟶ X) : f = g :=
  Quiver.Hom.op_inj (terminalIsTerminal.hom_ext (C := EJAObj.{u}ᵒᵖ) f.op g.op)

/-- The unique map `ℝᵤ ⟶ ejaTopO`. -/
def ejaUnitTop : ejaScal.{u} ⟶ ejaTopO.{u} := ejaUnitMapE ejaTopO.{u}

theorem ejaTop_inv₁ : ejaUnitTop.{u} ≫ ejaTopTo ejaScal.{u} = 𝟙 ejaScal.{u} :=
  (ejaUnitMapE_unique _).trans (ejaUnitMapE_unique _).symm

theorem ejaTop_inv₂ : ejaTopTo ejaScal.{u} ≫ ejaUnitTop.{u} = 𝟙 ejaTopO.{u} :=
  ejaTopO_hom_unique _ _

/-- `ℝ²` as an object of `EJAᵒᵖ`: the apex of the two-point presentation of
`⊤ + ⊤`. -/
abbrev ejaS : EJAObj.{u}ᵒᵖ := op (ejaProd ejaScal.{u} ejaScal.{u})

/-- The first point of `⊤ + ⊤` in `EJAᵒᵖ`. -/
def ejaI₁ : (⊤_ EJAObj.{u}ᵒᵖ) ⟶ ejaS.{u} :=
  Quiver.Hom.op (ejaFst ejaScal.{u} ejaScal.{u} ≫ ejaUnitTop.{u})

/-- The second point of `⊤ + ⊤` in `EJAᵒᵖ`. -/
def ejaI₂ : (⊤_ EJAObj.{u}ᵒᵖ) ⟶ ejaS.{u} :=
  Quiver.Hom.op (ejaSnd ejaScal.{u} ejaScal.{u} ≫ ejaUnitTop.{u})

/-- The swap of `ℝ²`, which will be the orthosupplement. -/
def ejaSwap : ejaS.{u} ⟶ ejaS.{u} :=
  Quiver.Hom.op (ejaPair (ejaSnd ejaScal.{u} ejaScal.{u})
    (ejaFst ejaScal.{u} ejaScal.{u}))

/-- **`⊤ + ⊤` in `EJAᵒᵖ` is `ℝ²`.** -/
def ejaTopCofan : IsColimit (BinaryCofan.mk ejaI₁.{u} ejaI₂.{u}) :=
  BinaryCofan.IsColimit.mk _
    (fun {_} u v => Quiver.Hom.op
      (ejaPair (u.unop ≫ ejaTopTo ejaScal.{u}) (v.unop ≫ ejaTopTo ejaScal.{u})))
    (fun {_} u v => by
      refine Quiver.Hom.unop_inj ?_
      show ejaPair (u.unop ≫ ejaTopTo ejaScal.{u}) (v.unop ≫ ejaTopTo ejaScal.{u})
          ≫ (ejaFst _ _ ≫ ejaUnitTop.{u}) = u.unop
      rw [← Category.assoc, ejaPair_fst, Category.assoc, ejaTop_inv₂, Category.comp_id])
    (fun {_} u v => by
      refine Quiver.Hom.unop_inj ?_
      show ejaPair (u.unop ≫ ejaTopTo ejaScal.{u}) (v.unop ≫ ejaTopTo ejaScal.{u})
          ≫ (ejaSnd _ _ ≫ ejaUnitTop.{u}) = v.unop
      rw [← Category.assoc, ejaPair_snd, Category.assoc, ejaTop_inv₂, Category.comp_id])
    (fun {W} u v m h₁ h₂ => by
      obtain ⟨m, rfl⟩ : ∃ m' : ejaS.{u} ⟶ W, m' = m := ⟨m, rfl⟩
      refine Quiver.Hom.unop_inj ?_
      have k₁ : m.unop ≫ (ejaFst ejaScal.{u} ejaScal.{u} ≫ ejaUnitTop.{u})
          = u.unop := congrArg Quiver.Hom.unop h₁
      have k₂ : m.unop ≫ (ejaSnd ejaScal.{u} ejaScal.{u} ≫ ejaUnitTop.{u})
          = v.unop := congrArg Quiver.Hom.unop h₂
      have e₁ : m.unop ≫ ejaFst ejaScal.{u} ejaScal.{u}
          = u.unop ≫ ejaTopTo ejaScal.{u} := by
        rw [← k₁, Category.assoc, Category.assoc, ejaTop_inv₁, Category.comp_id]
      have e₂ : m.unop ≫ ejaSnd ejaScal.{u} ejaScal.{u}
          = v.unop ≫ ejaTopTo ejaScal.{u} := by
        rw [← k₂, Category.assoc, Category.assoc, ejaTop_inv₁, Category.comp_id]
      rw [← e₁, ← e₂, ejaPair_eta]
      rfl)

theorem ejaI₁_swap : ejaI₁.{u} ≫ ejaSwap.{u} = ejaI₂.{u} := by
  refine Quiver.Hom.unop_inj ?_
  show ejaPair (ejaSnd ejaScal.{u} ejaScal.{u}) (ejaFst ejaScal.{u} ejaScal.{u})
      ≫ (ejaFst _ _ ≫ ejaUnitTop.{u}) = ejaSnd _ _ ≫ ejaUnitTop.{u}
  rw [← Category.assoc, ejaPair_fst]

theorem ejaI₂_swap : ejaI₂.{u} ≫ ejaSwap.{u} = ejaI₁.{u} := by
  refine Quiver.Hom.unop_inj ?_
  show ejaPair (ejaSnd ejaScal.{u} ejaScal.{u}) (ejaFst ejaScal.{u} ejaScal.{u})
      ≫ (ejaSnd _ _ ≫ ejaUnitTop.{u}) = ejaFst _ _ ≫ ejaUnitTop.{u}
  rw [← Category.assoc, ejaPair_snd]

/-- Positive unital maps `ℝ² ⟶ X` of `EJA` are the effects of `X`. -/
def ejaHomEffectEquiv (X : EJAObj.{u}) :
    (ejaProd ejaScal.{u} ejaScal.{u} ⟶ X)
      ≃ {x : X.obj.carrier // 0 ≤ x ∧ x ≤ X.obj.unit} :=
  ejaHomEquiv.trans (ousHomEffectEquiv X.obj)

/-- Passing to the opposite category. -/
def ejaOpEquiv (X : EJAObj.{u}) :
    ((op X : EJAObj.{u}ᵒᵖ) ⟶ ejaS.{u}) ≃ (ejaProd ejaScal.{u} ejaScal.{u} ⟶ X) where
  toFun f := f.unop
  invFun g := Quiver.Hom.op g
  left_inv _ := rfl
  right_inv _ := rfl

/-- **190V** (`effexamples`, eff.tex:2191, Examples): the **predicates on a
Euclidean Jordan algebra `E` correspond to its effects**, the elements
`0 ≤ a ≤ 1`.

By `eja_le_iff` and `eja_isSumSq_iff_exists_sq` these are exactly the `a` with
both `a` and `1 - a` squares. -/
def eja_pred_effect (X : EJAObj.{u}) :
    Pred (Par.of (op X)) ≃ {x : X.obj.carrier // 0 ≤ x ∧ x ≤ X.obj.unit} :=
  ((ouPredEquiv ejaTopCofan.{u} (op X)).trans (ejaOpEquiv X)).trans
    (ejaHomEffectEquiv X)

theorem eja_pred_effect_val (X : EJAObj.{u}) (p : Pred (Par.of (op X))) :
    ((eja_pred_effect X p).1 : X.obj.carrier)
      = (ouPredEquiv ejaTopCofan.{u} (op X) p).unop.hom.toLinearMap
          ((1 : ULift.{u} ℝ), (0 : ULift.{u} ℝ)) := rfl

theorem eja_from_i₁ (X : EJAObj.{u}) :
    terminal.from (op X) ≫ ejaI₁.{u}
      = Quiver.Hom.op (ejaFst ejaScal.{u} ejaScal.{u} ≫ ejaUnitMapE X) := by
  refine Quiver.Hom.unop_inj ?_
  show (ejaFst ejaScal.{u} ejaScal.{u} ≫ ejaUnitTop.{u}) ≫ ejaTopTo X
      = ejaFst ejaScal.{u} ejaScal.{u} ≫ ejaUnitMapE X
  rw [Category.assoc]
  exact congrArg (fun m => ejaFst ejaScal.{u} ejaScal.{u} ≫ m)
    (ejaUnitMapE_unique (ejaUnitTop.{u} ≫ ejaTopTo X))

theorem eja_from_i₂ (X : EJAObj.{u}) :
    terminal.from (op X) ≫ ejaI₂.{u}
      = Quiver.Hom.op (ejaSnd ejaScal.{u} ejaScal.{u} ≫ ejaUnitMapE X) := by
  refine Quiver.Hom.unop_inj ?_
  show (ejaSnd ejaScal.{u} ejaScal.{u} ≫ ejaUnitTop.{u}) ≫ ejaTopTo X
      = ejaSnd ejaScal.{u} ejaScal.{u} ≫ ejaUnitMapE X
  rw [Category.assoc]
  exact congrArg (fun m => ejaSnd ejaScal.{u} ejaScal.{u} ≫ m)
    (ejaUnitMapE_unique (ejaUnitTop.{u} ≫ ejaTopTo X))

/-- **190V**: the truth predicate is the Jordan unit. -/
theorem eja_pred_effect_truth (X : EJAObj.{u}) :
    ((eja_pred_effect X (truth (Par.of (op X)))).1 : X.obj.carrier) = X.obj.unit := by
  rw [eja_pred_effect_val, ouPredEquiv_truth, eja_from_i₁]
  show (ousUnitMap X.obj).toLinearMap (1 : ULift.{u} ℝ) = X.obj.unit
  rw [ousUnitMap_apply]
  exact one_smul _ _

/-- **190V**: the zero predicate is `0`. -/
theorem eja_pred_effect_zero (X : EJAObj.{u}) :
    ((eja_pred_effect X (0 : Pred (Par.of (op X)))).1 : X.obj.carrier) = 0 := by
  rw [eja_pred_effect_val, ouPredEquiv_zero, eja_from_i₂]
  show (ousUnitMap X.obj).toLinearMap (0 : ULift.{u} ℝ) = 0
  rw [ousUnitMap_apply]
  exact zero_smul _ _

/-- **190V**: the orthosupplement of a predicate is `1 - a`. -/
theorem eja_pred_effect_orth (X : EJAObj.{u}) (p : Pred (Par.of (op X))) :
    ((eja_pred_effect X (orth p)).1 : X.obj.carrier)
      = X.obj.unit - ((eja_pred_effect X p).1 : X.obj.carrier) := by
  rw [eja_pred_effect_val, eja_pred_effect_val,
    ouPredEquiv_orth ejaTopCofan.{u} ejaSwap.{u} ejaI₁_swap.{u} ejaI₂_swap.{u} (op X) p]
  exact ous_hom_compl X.obj (ouPredEquiv ejaTopCofan.{u} (op X) p).unop.hom

/-- Maps `X ⟶ ℝᵤ` of `EJA` are the positive unital linear functionals. -/
def ejaHomStateEquiv (X : EJAObj.{u}) :
    (X ⟶ ejaScal.{u})
      ≃ {f : X.obj.carrier →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ // (∀ x, 0 ≤ x → 0 ≤ f x) ∧ f X.obj.unit = 1} :=
  ejaHomEquiv.trans (ousHomStateEquiv X.obj)

/-- `ejaTopO ≅ ℝᵤ`, as a bijection on maps into it. -/
def ejaHomTopEquiv (X : EJAObj.{u}) : (X ⟶ ejaTopO.{u}) ≃ (X ⟶ ejaScal.{u}) where
  toFun f := f ≫ ejaTopTo ejaScal.{u}
  invFun g := g ≫ ejaUnitTop.{u}
  left_inv f := by
    show (f ≫ ejaTopTo ejaScal.{u}) ≫ ejaUnitTop.{u} = f
    rw [Category.assoc, ejaTop_inv₂, Category.comp_id]
  right_inv g := by
    show (g ≫ ejaUnitTop.{u}) ≫ ejaTopTo ejaScal.{u} = g
    rw [Category.assoc, ejaTop_inv₁, Category.comp_id]

/-- **190V** (`effexamples`, eff.tex:2194, Examples): the **states of a
Euclidean Jordan algebra are exactly what are usually considered states for
EJAs**: the positive unit-preserving linear functionals. -/
def eja_stat_state (X : EJAObj.{u}) :
    Stat (Par.of (op X))
      ≃ {f : X.obj.carrier →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ // (∀ x, 0 ≤ x → 0 ≤ f x) ∧ f X.obj.unit = 1} :=
  ((parStatEquiv (op X)).trans
    { toFun := fun f => f.unop
      invFun := fun g => Quiver.Hom.op g
      left_inv := fun _ => rfl
      right_inv := fun _ => rfl }).trans
    ((ejaHomTopEquiv X).trans (ejaHomStateEquiv X))

end EJAExample


/-! ## The ⋄-effectus clause of 206III, and what it would take

206III (eff.tex:4460, Examples) says that `vNᵒᵖ`, `CvNᵒᵖ`, `EJAᵒᵖ` and
`Set` are all ⋄-effectuses.  The `EJAᵒᵖ` clause is *not* carried here, and
the reason is not the effectus axioms — it is that a ⋄-effectus lives in
the **partial** form, which needs a different category and a theory of
Jordan compressions that neither this tree nor Mathlib has.  Precisely:

* `DiamondEffectus` (`DiamondAmp.lean`) presupposes, as instances,
  `HasFiniteCoproducts`, a `PCM` on every hom-set, `FinPAC` and
  `EffectusPartialForm`.  For von Neumann algebras those live on the
  *subunital* category `WStarCPSU` (`WStarCat.lean`), not on the unital
  one; the corresponding `EJA_psu` — Euclidean Jordan algebras with
  positive **subunital** maps — does not exist here, and the vN precedent
  (`VNExamples.lean`, `section VNPartial`) puts that infrastructure at
  about 500 lines, plus about 180 for the bridge that identifies the
  predicates on an object with its effects `0 ≤ a ≤ 1`.
* On top of that come the four obligations of a ⋄-effectus, about 230
  lines for `vNᵒᵖ`, and each needs a piece of Jordan theory that is
  missing: comprehension needs the *compression* `U_p` onto the Peirce-1
  subalgebra of an idempotent `p` together with the fact that this corner
  is again a Euclidean Jordan algebra (the vN analogue is the standard
  corner `b ↦ ⌊a⌋ b ⌊a⌋`, `Dils/Pure.lean`); quotients need the filter
  `b ↦ U_{√(1 - a)} b`, hence *square roots*, hence a continuous
  functional calculus; images need the range (support) projection of a
  positive map and the ceiling `⌈a⌉` of an effect; and `orth_sharp` needs
  that the sharp predicates are exactly the idempotents, so that `1 - p`
  is sharp when `p` is.
* All four rest on the **spectral theorem for a single element** of a
  formally real Jordan algebra — `x` generates an associative,
  finite-dimensional real algebra, hence a copy of `ℝᵏ`.  That prerequisite
  is *no longer* missing: it is `eja_spectral` above, together with
  power-associativity (`eja_pow_mul_pow`), the trace form (`ejaB`) and the
  Peirce relation `2 L p ³ - 3 L p ² + L p = 0` for an idempotent
  (`eja_idem_cubic`, `ejaPone`, `ejaPhalf`).  What is still missing for the
  four obligations is the *quadratic representation* `U_a = 2 L a ² - L a²`
  and the fundamental formula `U_(U_a b) = U_a U_b U_a`, from which the
  compression onto the Peirce-1 subalgebra of an idempotent, the square
  root of an effect and the range projection of a positive map are built;
  Mathlib has none of that, and this file builds only the pieces of Peirce
  theory that the spectral theorem itself needs.

Nothing in the total-form part of this file depends on any of that: an
effectus in *total* form needs only the order unit space structure, which
is what `toOrderUnitSpace` supplies. -/

end

end Theses.B.Eff

